WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:01.570
MS.

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:02.570
HARF: Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:03.570
Welcome to the daily briefing.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.320
I do not have anything at the top, so I will
go to your questions.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:12.769
QUESTION: Can you just go through for us the
phone calls that the Secretary had yesterday

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:16.450
and any that he might have had today, specifically
related to Syria?

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:17.450
MS.

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.450
HARF: Absolutely.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:23.850
Yesterday, the Secretary spoke with the UN
Secretary General Ban Ki-moon, with the Arab

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:29.429
League Secretary General Elaraby, with EU
High Representative Ashton, with the Egyptian

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:34.350
Foreign Minister Fahmy, with Turkish Foreign
Minister Davutoglu, Emirati Foreign Minister

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:40.020
Abdallah bin Zayid, Saudi Foreign Minister
Saud al-Faysal, NATO Secretary General Rasmussen,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:45.750
Qatari Foreign Minister al-Attiyah, two calls
with Jordanian Foreign Minister Judeh, a call

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.870
with the UK Foreign Secretary Hague, and another
call -- excuse me -- with the Arab League

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:50.870
Secretary General.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.730
Those were all yesterday.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:53.730
QUESTION: So --

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:54.730
MS.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:55.730
HARF: So --

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:56.730
QUESTION: Sorry.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:57.730
Yesterday, two with Elaraby?

00:00:57.730 --> 00:00:58.730
MS.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:00:59.730
HARF: Correct, yes.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:00.730
QUESTION: And two with --

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.730
MS.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:02.730
HARF: -- with Judeh.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:03.730
QUESTION: With Judeh.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.730
MS.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:05.730
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:06.730
QUESTION: And then one each with the others
that you --

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:07.730
MS.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:08.730
HARF: Correct, yes.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:09.730
And then today, so far on my list -- and just
for planning purposes, I think for a few days

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:10.730
we'll send an update at the end of the day
with all of the list of calls that we're doing

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:11.730
on this.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:15.110
But so far today, he's spoken with the Russian
Foreign Minister Lavrov and with the Moroccan

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:16.510
Foreign Minister as well.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:17.510
QUESTION: Okay.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.430
Could I just ask: Could you make an exception
to that end of the day grouping if he happens

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.830
to call Assad or Muallim?

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:24.830
MS.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:25.830
HARF: Yes.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:26.830
QUESTION: Okay.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:27.900
Thank you.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.210
You've seen the Arab League statement from
this morning.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:32.210
MS.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.210
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.210
QUESTION: I'm wondering what you have -- if
you have any reaction/response to it.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.210
MS.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.210
HARF: Yes.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:40.240
Well, I think it was a very strong statement
that made a few points, and let me pull those

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.240
up right now.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:45.290
I know you all have seen it as well: sharply
condemned the use of banned chemical weapons,

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:49.290
something, of course, that we agree with;
called for holding the Syrian regime fully

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.740
responsible for this crime.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.270
And I think it would be important to say at
the top that we believe that any careful review

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:01.200
of the facts in this situation leads to the
conclusion that the regime was, in fact, behind

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.250
this horrific chemical weapons attack.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:06.800
It's undeniable that chemical weapons were
used here on a large scale.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.550
We know that the regime maintains custody
of those weapons and uses these types of rockets,

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.850
and we also know that the opposition does
not have those capabilities.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:19.140
We'll have additional information forthcoming
from the intelligence community in the days

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.260
to come on this assessment.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:26.950
QUESTION: Would you say that in the Secretary's
phone calls yesterday and the one today with

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:27.950
the Moroccan --

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:28.950
MS.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:29.950
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:36.890
QUESTION: -- I mean the calls yesterday particularly
to the Arab foreign ministers and to Elaraby,

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:44.440
that this statement or the meeting that this
statement emanated from was a subject of conversation?

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:45.440
MS.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.820
HARF: I don't know if the statement was a
subject of their conversations.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:48.820
QUESTION: No.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:49.820
The meeting.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:50.820
MS.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.570
HARF: I don't know if the meetings specifically
-- I know the Arab League remains in emergency

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:53.570
session.

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:59.760
The Secretary in all of these calls has been
discussing clearly the assessment that we're

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.580
undertaking and how we will at some point
be making a decision about a response.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:06.000
I don't have more specifics in terms of those
calls.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.000
QUESTION: Okay.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.740
So I'm just curious: Do you have -- I mean,
you said it was a very strong statement.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.610
Is the Administration pleased with what the
Arab League had to say?

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:15.610
MS.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.760
HARF: I think we -- both when it comes to
the Arab League, but anybody else -- would

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:23.441
encourage people and organizations to make
strong statements condemning the use of chemical

00:03:23.441 --> 00:03:24.950
weapons in this case and all others.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:25.950
QUESTION: Well, can I --

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:26.950
QUESTION: What -- I mean, just --

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:27.950
QUESTION: Can I jump in?

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:28.950
QUESTION: Let me just -- let me just --

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:29.950
MS.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:30.950
HARF: Let me let Matt finish.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:31.950
QUESTION: I just have one more on this statement
--

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:32.950
MS.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:33.950
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:34.950
QUESTION: -- and then Elise can take over.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:35.950
The statement also urged the -- called on
the Security Council to overcome its differences

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.510
-- the members of the Security Council to
overcome their differences and to take deterrent

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.510
action against this.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:39.510
Is this something the United States feels
is necessary before any kind of a response

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.510
is implemented?

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:41.510
MS.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:58.080
HARF: Well, I would underscore again today
that the President has not yet made a decision

00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.080
on --

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.080
QUESTION: I'm not -- I know.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.080
Go ahead.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.080
MS.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:03.080
HARF: Okay.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:04.080
Thank you.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.080
I would underscore that the President has
not yet made a decision yet, so I'm not going

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.040
to hypothetically talk about what may or may
not be part of that decision.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.510
I know there are a lot of rumors out there
about whether we'll take Action A or Action

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:13.470
B and what that might entail.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:18.000
Clearly, the Secretary and the President and
others remain in close consultation with our

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.709
international partners and allies.

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.750
We've read out a lot of those calls.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:23.760
That consultation will continue.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.880
QUESTION: I'm not asking what the response
is going to be.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:27.880
MS.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:28.880
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.250
QUESTION: But in the range of responses that
the Administration is considering, do any

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:36.750
-- would any of them, in the opinion of the
Administration, require action by the UN Security

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:37.750
Council?

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:38.750
MS.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:40.630
HARF: I'm not going to answer that question
one way or the other before the President's

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:41.630
made a decision.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:42.630
QUESTION: Okay.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:48.200
So on this statement, did you -- was this
statement kind of formulated as a result of

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.330
you sharing?

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:55.250
Because they clearly hold the regime accountable
-- holding the regime fully accountable for

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:56.630
this horrible crime.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:05:01.660
Was that -- were you sharing your intelligence
assessments that you said you'll share with

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:02.660
the public?

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:06.600
Have you been sharing those intelligence assessments
with the Arab League as they formulated their

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.660
statement that was very kind of undoubtedly
pointing the finger at the regime?

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:09.660
MS.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.600
HARF: Well, I would say a couple points.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.050
Broadly speaking, we are sharing our assessment
with our allies and partners around the world

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.050
as we make them.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:22.080
I don't know, to be honest with you, if that
was part of what went into the Arab League

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.080
statement.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.490
I think you'd have to check with them about
what helped them formulate their statement.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:31.700
But I think it's crystal clear to anybody
looking at this that the regime was responsible

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:32.700
here.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.890
QUESTION: Well, it's crystal clear circumstantially,
but it would have to -- you -- as you said,

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:41.690
that you would put out your intelligence assessment
that would prove what you're saying is undeniable.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:44.740
So I mean, I guess we'll be waiting for that.

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:50.910
But my question is: Did you ask the Arab League
to issue some kind of statement that would

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:56.780
show that the Arabs hold the regime accountable
for that?

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:57.780
MS.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.510
HARF: Well, clearly the Arab League makes
decisions about its statements --

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:00.510
QUESTION: I understand that.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:01.510
But did you --

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:02.510
MS.

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:03.510
HARF: Can I finish?

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:04.510
QUESTION: Yeah, sure.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:05.510
MS.

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:06.510
HARF: Okay.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:07.510
Thank you.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:08.510
Clearly, the Arab League can make decisions
about its statements on its own.

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:11.889
We have called publicly and privately on everybody
to strongly condemn what happened in Syria

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:14.270
and to make it clear that we need to hold
the regime accountable.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:18.110
So I'm not going to specifically outline what
the Secretary's discussions were with the

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:23.340
Secretary General, other than to say that,
clearly, we call on people and organizations

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.070
around the world to make such strong statements.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:31.980
QUESTION: Do you see this statement as an
endorsement of or in support of any action

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.180
that you say that you -- you say that there
will be a response.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.180
MS.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:37.180
HARF: Correct.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.250
QUESTION: Do you see this as a call by the
Arab League for some kind of international

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.250
response?

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.250
MS.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.070
HARF: Well, they very clearly said that they
need -- the regime needed to be held accountable,

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:48.810
and we've been -- said that we will respond
appropriately as the President makes a decision.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.960
So I think that is pretty crystal clear in
their statement.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:51.960
QUESTION: So --

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:52.960
QUESTION: So --

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.300
QUESTION: So if the U.S. were to take a response,
then you see this Arab statement as a kind

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.890
of -- I don't want to say "green light," but
a --

00:06:58.890 --> 00:06:59.890
MS.

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:01.242
HARF: I think you're taking it a step further.

00:07:01.242 --> 00:07:02.760
The President hasn't made a decision yet,
and I wouldn't --

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.050
QUESTION: He hasn't made a decision on what
he's going to do, but --

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.050
MS.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.050
HARF: Okay.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:09.050
QUESTION: -- Secretary Kerry, Secretary Hagel
talking about --

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.050
MS.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.050
HARF: Yes.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.290
QUESTION: -- they're ready to go like that,
it's pretty clear that the Administration

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:16.660
is kind of putting out there that there is
going to be some kind of response, whether

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:17.660
it's military or something else.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:18.660
MS.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:19.660
HARF: I'm saying there's going to be a response.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:20.660
QUESTION: Okay.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:21.660
MS.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:22.660
HARF: Nobody is hiding that.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:23.660
QUESTION: Okay.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:24.660
So do you --

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:25.660
MS.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:26.660
HARF: What I was going to say before you interrupted
me was that the President hasn't made a decision

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:27.660
yet.

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:28.740
I wouldn't read into this Arab League statement
a specific endorsement of a specific decision

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:29.870
that has not yet been made.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:30.870
That's a step further.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.360
QUESTION: Well, a decision has been made to
do something.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:33.360
MS.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.360
HARF: Correct.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:35.360
But the decision about what has not yet been
made.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:41.530
QUESTION: Do you see this as any kind of legal
support from the Arab League?

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:42.530
MS.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.510
HARF: I'm not going to do any sort of legal
analysis today about a hypothetical decision

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:46.510
that has yet to be made.

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:47.510
QUESTION: So --

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:48.510
MS.

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:49.510
HARF: Yes, Lesley.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:50.510
QUESTION: Can I have one?

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:51.510
Was the Secretary meeting with the President
this morning?

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:52.510
MS.

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:54.970
HARF: Not to my knowledge.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:55.970
I can double-check on his schedule.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:56.970
QUESTION: Okay.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:57.970
MS.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:07:58.970
HARF: Not to my knowledge, no.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:07:59.970
QUESTION: Then would the --

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:00.970
MS.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:02.220
HARF: He clearly remains -- let me just say
he clearly remains in close contact with senior

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:06.669
officials in the White House, and if we have
any meetings to read out or the White House

00:08:06.669 --> 00:08:07.669
has to read out, we can do so.

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:08.669
QUESTION: Are you --

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:09.669
QUESTION: What was the reason for postponing
that meeting with the Russians?

00:08:09.669 --> 00:08:20.571
Do you feel that there is just nothing to
be said right now on the political process,

00:08:20.571 --> 00:08:25.600
that a response is imminent or -- not your
words -- that a response is to be soon and

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.889
that that just has to be on hold for now and
that you wouldn't make any gains from a meeting

00:08:29.889 --> 00:08:30.889
like that?

00:08:30.889 --> 00:08:31.889
MS.

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:35.391
HARF: Well, given our ongoing consultations
about an appropriate response to the chemical

00:08:35.391 --> 00:08:39.460
weapons attack, we have decided to postpone
this week's meeting that was supposed to happen

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:40.570
in The Hague.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:44.490
We want it to happen at the time when we have
the best chance to make progress.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.920
We are working now with the Russians to reschedule
it.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.089
I would underscore strongly that this is only
a postponement; it is not a cancelation of

00:08:51.089 --> 00:08:52.089
the meeting.

00:08:52.089 --> 00:08:56.560
And I think that what happened on August 21st
only underscores the need for a political

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:03.750
solution, and we remain fully invested in
a comprehensive and durable political solution

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:08.200
to the crisis in Syria because we continue
to believe that there is no military solution

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.200
to this crisis.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.630
QUESTION: So what kind of message would you
be sending by cutting off those, because some

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:16.540
people think that you feel -- you should keep
those diplomatic lines open?

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:19.140
And clearly there are calls, but there's nothing
better than face-to-face meetings.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:20.140
MS.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.840
HARF: Well, as I said, the Secretary spoke
with the Russian Foreign Minister today, so

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:27.130
his level of consultation on this issue is
quite high and sustained and will continue

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:28.130
to be.

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.570
We did not believe that this was the right
timing for this meeting, but again, we are

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:35.900
working right now with the Russians to reschedule
it at a time that makes more sense, hopefully

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:37.470
in the very near future.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:40.890
And we'll continue working with Russia and
our international partners, again, because

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:42.500
we are fully invested in this process.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:43.500
QUESTION: Marie?

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:44.500
MS.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:45.500
HARF: Yes.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:48.070
QUESTION: On Elise's point, I mean, surely
it's very important because there is a precedent.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:53.540
Back in 1990, the Arab League met and basically
did exactly the same thing, giving a green

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.340
light for what became known as Desert Storm.

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:56.750
So you are aware of that, are you not?

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:57.750
MS.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:58.790
HARF: I'm aware of the history, Said.

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:04.310
I'm not going to get ahead of where we are
and make hypothetical legal analysis about

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:05.560
a decision that hasn't been made yet.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:06.560
QUESTION: Right.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:07.710
But surely you see this as some sort of an
endorsement.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:08.710
Is it not?

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:09.710
MS.

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:10.710
HARF: I'm not going to use that word, Said.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:11.710
QUESTION: Okay, all right.

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:12.710
MS.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:13.710
HARF: We think it's a strong statement, and
I'm not going to go any further.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:14.710
QUESTION: But I mean --

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:16.060
QUESTION: On the issue -- on the issue of
crystal clear, because you keep saying crystal

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.060
clear --

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:18.060
MS.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:19.060
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.930
QUESTION: -- but so far, what we have seen
are videos supplied by a party to the combat,

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:26.300
when we have seen that you keep stating that
only the Syrian regime has the rocket capability

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:27.300
and so on.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:31.560
So it all seems to be conjecture or it seems
to be circumstantial, not really -- not something

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:32.560
--

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:33.560
MS.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.410
HARF: It's not circumstantial evidence that
the regime maintains custody of these kind

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:37.410
of weapons.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:38.410
We have a broad --

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:39.410
QUESTION: Right.

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:40.410
MS.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.130
HARF: -- array of information that points
to that, that they are the only ones that

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:45.850
have the capability to use these kind of rockets
and delivery systems, that the opposition

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:48.420
does not have those capabilities.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.730
And I would add to that, before I get to your
next question, that we've already had a high

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:56.060
confidence assessment from our intelligence
community that the regime has used chemical

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:57.300
weapons during this conflict.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:02.260
So as I said, the intelligence community will
be sharing additional information about what

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:06.830
underlies this assessment, but I think that
any assertion that the opposition could be

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:08.180
behind this just defies logic.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:09.180
QUESTION: Okay.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:14.660
So from your point of view, what useful purpose
are the UN investigators serving if you have

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:21.089
already arrived at a beyond-a-shadow-of-any-doubt
conclusion that the regime has already used

00:11:21.089 --> 00:11:22.410
chemical weapons?

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:24.130
What useful purpose do they serve, and why
do they continue to be there?

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:25.130
MS.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.130
HARF: Could the UN investigators serve?

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:27.130
QUESTION: Yes, ma'am.

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:30.770
Because apparently, you have already arrived
at that conclusion before they submit their

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:31.770
report.

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:32.770
MS.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:33.800
HARF: Well, I'd make a couple points.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:38.290
The first, as I said yesterday, was let's
keep in mind what the mandate of the UN investigation

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:39.290
team is.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:40.400
It's not to determine culpability.

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:44.830
It's to determine whether chemical weapons
are used, which, again, they're -- that was

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:47.890
determined very, very shortly after it happened.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:53.300
And that even today we saw the area the UN
investigation was supposed to go visit is

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.540
being attacked by the Syrian regime, so they've
had to postpone their investigation.

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:01.029
So we think it's important that the regime
not be able to use the investigation as a

00:12:01.029 --> 00:12:05.490
stalling tactic or a charade to hide behind
under this notion that they somehow didn't

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:06.490
perpetrate this attack.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:13.180
QUESTION: So why then -- why go through this
exercise of sending the inspectors, the UN

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:18.630
inspectors, if you already concluded that
there was use of chemical weapons and they

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.529
are not to determine who did what, correct?

00:12:20.529 --> 00:12:21.529
MS.

00:12:21.529 --> 00:12:24.190
HARF: Well, we clearly value the UN's work
-- we've said that from the beginning -- when

00:12:24.190 --> 00:12:26.700
it comes to investigating chemical weapons
in Syria.

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:31.140
And it's obviously up to the UN to make their
decisions about their team and when they will

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:34.120
attempt to go investigate things and when
they won't.

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:39.220
But we've reached a point now where we believe
too much time has passed for the investigation

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:44.130
to be credible and that it's clear the security
situation isn't safe for the team in Syria.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:49.420
So again, we don't want the regime to be able
to use the UN investigation as a stalling

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:51.130
tactic to hide behind.

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:54.410
QUESTION: So does that mean that you think
that they should leave?

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:55.410
MS.

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:56.410
HARF: Well --

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:58.180
QUESTION: That they shouldn't carry on with
-- I mean, do you think that what they are

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:04.350
doing or not doing, as the case may be, since
they seem to be stuck in their hotel, is a

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:08.380
stalling tactic and therefore they should
be pulled out of the country?

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:09.380
MS.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:11.740
HARF: Well, the Secretary has been in close
contact with the Secretary General of the

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:12.740
UN.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:15.339
I'm not going to specifically detail what
those conversations outlined.

00:13:15.339 --> 00:13:21.440
We've made our views known publicly and privately
that we believe that the UN investigation:

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:26.250
(a) It's not safe for them on the ground;
and (b) that we believe too long of a time

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:28.279
has passed for them to be -- for it to be
credible.

00:13:28.279 --> 00:13:29.279
QUESTION: So they --

00:13:29.279 --> 00:13:30.279
MS.

00:13:30.279 --> 00:13:31.279
HARF: And we've made those views known.

00:13:31.279 --> 00:13:34.480
QUESTION: So you're not pushing for them to
go back and look at some of these areas where

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:35.480
the weapons are supposedly have used?

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:36.480
MS.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:37.480
HARF: Well --

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:38.480
QUESTION: You don't see the point to it?

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:39.480
MS.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:40.480
HARF: We believe that it's too late to be
credible, correct.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:41.480
QUESTION: Okay.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:42.480
I'm just --

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:43.480
MS.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:44.480
HARF: But clearly, they make their own decisions
about their team and --

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:45.480
QUESTION: I understand.

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.750
But I mean, you don't think that it would
be helpful for them to present a report, especially

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:52.180
considering the fact that they're not going
to determine culpability even in the best-case

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:53.240
scenario?

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:58.420
That would not be helpful in trying to bring
about people like the Russians who -- helpful

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.730
in convincing them that this has actually
happened?

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:02.730
MS.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:15.625
HARF: We do believe that it's too late to
be credible.

00:14:15.625 --> 00:14:16.625
QUESTION: Okay.

00:14:16.625 --> 00:14:17.625
And then just --

00:14:17.625 --> 00:14:18.625
QUESTION: But to the -- I just --

00:14:18.625 --> 00:14:19.625
MS.

00:14:19.625 --> 00:14:20.625
HARF: I'll get to you in one second.

00:14:20.625 --> 00:14:21.625
QUESTION: Just can I ask --

00:14:21.625 --> 00:14:22.625
MS.

00:14:22.625 --> 00:14:23.625
HARF: Yes, to you and then you.

00:14:23.625 --> 00:14:24.625
QUESTION: I just want to follow up on the
Lavrov call.

00:14:24.625 --> 00:14:25.625
MS.

00:14:25.625 --> 00:14:26.625
HARF: And then you.

00:14:26.625 --> 00:14:27.625
QUESTION: And then me.

00:14:27.625 --> 00:14:28.625
QUESTION: Did the Secretary and Foreign Minister
Lavrov, do you know, did they discuss this

00:14:28.625 --> 00:14:29.625
postponed meeting?

00:14:29.625 --> 00:14:30.625
MS.

00:14:30.625 --> 00:14:31.625
HARF: I don't know the answer.

00:14:31.625 --> 00:14:32.625
QUESTION: The reason I ask is that I'm just
wondering: When were the Russians told that

00:14:32.625 --> 00:14:33.625
you guys would not be sending anyone to The
Hague?

00:14:33.625 --> 00:14:34.625
MS.

00:14:34.625 --> 00:14:35.625
HARF: I don't know the answer.

00:14:35.625 --> 00:14:36.625
I believe it was last night.

00:14:36.625 --> 00:14:37.625
QUESTION: Okay.

00:14:37.625 --> 00:14:38.625
MS.

00:14:38.625 --> 00:14:39.625
HARF: I do not know if they discussed it today.

00:14:39.625 --> 00:14:40.625
Let me check and see if I can share (inaudible)
on that.

00:14:40.625 --> 00:14:41.625
QUESTION: Okay.

00:14:41.625 --> 00:14:42.625
And then with your quibble over cancelation
versus postponement --

00:14:42.625 --> 00:14:43.625
MS.

00:14:43.625 --> 00:14:44.625
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:14:44.625 --> 00:14:45.625
QUESTION: -- this meeting was to be held on
the 28th, correct?

00:14:45.625 --> 00:14:46.625
MS.

00:14:46.625 --> 00:14:47.625
HARF: Correct.

00:14:47.625 --> 00:14:48.625
It's been postponed --

00:14:48.625 --> 00:14:49.625
QUESTION: All right.

00:14:49.625 --> 00:14:50.625
And --

00:14:50.625 --> 00:14:51.625
MS.

00:14:51.625 --> 00:14:52.625
HARF: And they are working to reschedule it
for an additional date.

00:14:52.625 --> 00:14:53.625
QUESTION: Right.

00:14:53.625 --> 00:14:54.625
But the meeting is no longer happening on
the 28th.

00:14:54.625 --> 00:14:55.625
Is that correct?

00:14:55.625 --> 00:14:56.625
MS.

00:14:56.625 --> 00:14:57.625
HARF: Correct.

00:14:57.625 --> 00:14:58.625
QUESTION: So there is no --

00:14:58.625 --> 00:14:59.625
MS.

00:14:59.625 --> 00:15:00.625
HARF: We're going to do definitions now, aren't
we?

00:15:00.625 --> 00:15:01.625
QUESTION: I just -- I just --

00:15:01.625 --> 00:15:02.625
MS.

00:15:02.625 --> 00:15:03.625
HARF: I'm ready for it.

00:15:03.625 --> 00:15:04.625
QUESTION: I'm just not sure I understand what
your --

00:15:04.625 --> 00:15:05.625
MS.

00:15:05.625 --> 00:15:06.625
HARF: Well, there is a --

00:15:06.625 --> 00:15:07.625
QUESTION: -- what your quibble is.

00:15:07.625 --> 00:15:08.625
Because if there was going to be a meeting
that was scheduled on the 28th, then we can

00:15:08.625 --> 00:15:09.625
go back to meetings, say, in other cities,
like Jericho for example, but if there was

00:15:09.625 --> 00:15:10.625
going to be a meeting --

00:15:10.625 --> 00:15:11.625
MS.

00:15:11.625 --> 00:15:12.625
HARF: Just throwing one out there.

00:15:12.625 --> 00:15:13.625
Yes.

00:15:13.625 --> 00:15:14.625
QUESTION: If there was going to be a meeting
and then you guys decided not to have it,

00:15:14.625 --> 00:15:15.625
if there was going to be a meeting on a certain
date and you decided not to have it --

00:15:15.625 --> 00:15:16.625
MS.

00:15:16.625 --> 00:15:17.625
HARF: I do think the word difference is important
here --

00:15:17.625 --> 00:15:18.625
QUESTION: Okay.

00:15:18.625 --> 00:15:19.625
MS.

00:15:19.625 --> 00:15:20.625
HARF: -- and let me tell you why.

00:15:20.625 --> 00:15:21.625
QUESTION: But wait a second.

00:15:21.625 --> 00:15:22.625
MS.

00:15:22.625 --> 00:15:23.625
HARF: Can I tell you why?

00:15:23.625 --> 00:15:24.625
QUESTION: The meeting that was on the 28th
--

00:15:24.625 --> 00:15:25.625
MS.

00:15:25.625 --> 00:15:26.625
HARF: Yes.

00:15:26.625 --> 00:15:27.625
QUESTION: -- of August --

00:15:27.625 --> 00:15:28.625
MS.

00:15:28.625 --> 00:15:29.625
HARF: -- is not happening.

00:15:29.625 --> 00:15:30.625
QUESTION: -- is not happening.

00:15:30.625 --> 00:15:31.625
MS.

00:15:31.625 --> 00:15:32.625
HARF: It is post --

00:15:32.625 --> 00:15:33.625
QUESTION: That --

00:15:33.625 --> 00:15:34.625
MS.

00:15:34.625 --> 00:15:35.625
HARF: The same meeting will be happening very
soon at a date we determine with the Russians.

00:15:35.625 --> 00:15:36.625
QUESTION: Okay.

00:15:36.625 --> 00:15:37.625
And did -- was that a subject of the conversation
with Foreign Minister Lavrov this morning?

00:15:37.625 --> 00:15:38.625
MS.

00:15:38.625 --> 00:15:39.625
HARF: I do not know.

00:15:39.625 --> 00:15:40.625
I can attempt to find out.

00:15:40.625 --> 00:15:41.625
I don't know.

00:15:41.625 --> 00:15:42.625
But the reason I think it's important, and
let me underscore this, is because I think

00:15:42.625 --> 00:15:43.625
there have been some people who have been
taking from this postponement that maybe we're

00:15:43.625 --> 00:15:44.625
not fully invested in the Geneva process,
that we're walking away from the process with

00:15:44.625 --> 00:15:45.625
the Russians, even though, clearly, it's a
difficult time and a difficult situation.

00:15:45.625 --> 00:15:48.620
And nothing could actually be further from
the truth, so I want to underscore that we

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:53.150
are fully invested and working with the Russians
even as we disagree on a lot in Syria today.

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:54.150
QUESTION: Okay.

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:55.150
MS.

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:56.150
HARF: And I think that actually is a very
important point.

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:57.890
QUESTION: So you might say from this podium
that you could assure us that no meetings

00:15:57.890 --> 00:15:58.890
had been canceled.

00:15:58.890 --> 00:15:59.890
QUESTION: (Laughter.)

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:00.890
Sorry.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:01.890
QUESTION: Is that correct?

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:02.890
(Laughter.)

00:16:02.890 --> 00:16:03.890
MS.

00:16:03.890 --> 00:16:04.890
HARF: Matt, I'm not even going to go there
today.

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:05.890
Not even going to go there.

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:06.890
This meeting has been postponed.

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:07.890
But I appreciate the effort.

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:08.890
That was a good one.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:09.890
QUESTION: Marie, now that it has been established
--

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:10.890
MS.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:11.890
HARF: I'll get to you next.

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:12.890
Go ahead, Said.

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:13.890
Yes.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:17.640
QUESTION: -- beyond a shadow of a doubt that
chemical weapons were used, do we know what

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.130
kind -- what type of chemical weapons were
used?

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:21.290
Is it sarin?

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:22.290
Is it nerve gas?

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:23.290
Is it even worse than that?

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:24.290
MS.

00:16:24.290 --> 00:16:25.290
HARF: I don't have --

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:26.290
QUESTION: What is it?

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:27.290
MS.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:28.290
HARF: I don't have additional details about
that, Said.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:30.890
Again, the intelligence community will be
sharing as many additional details as we can

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:32.029
in the coming days.

00:16:32.029 --> 00:16:33.550
QUESTION: Okay.

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:37.089
Are we likely to hear like a statement perhaps
in the -- maybe today, maybe tomorrow -- that

00:16:37.089 --> 00:16:40.829
-- at least some sort of an intelligence report
that is declassified or can be announced?

00:16:40.829 --> 00:16:42.769
Is that likely to happen?

00:16:42.769 --> 00:16:43.769
MS.

00:16:43.769 --> 00:16:46.260
HARF: I don't have any specifics on timing.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:50.160
I will say that once the intelligence community
is prepared to make its formal assessment,

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:54.980
we will provide a classified assessment to
the Congress, and we will make an - unclassified

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.810
details available to the public.

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:01.800
I can say that we will certainly be making
those findings public this week.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:03.920
I don't have additional timeline beyond that.

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:08.019
QUESTION: But according to the American media
that the United States could hit Syria as

00:17:08.019 --> 00:17:11.609
early as Thursday was posed by a senior official.

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:14.089
So do you have any comments on that?

00:17:14.089 --> 00:17:19.129
And some analysts say, if the strike happens,
United States could take a different form

00:17:19.129 --> 00:17:21.199
of strike from the Iraq War -- maybe drone
or (inaudible) missiles first?

00:17:21.199 --> 00:17:22.199
MS.

00:17:22.199 --> 00:18:13.169
HARF: Well, I'd make two points, the first
being that the President hasn't made a decision

00:18:13.169 --> 00:18:14.169
yet on how to respond.

00:18:14.169 --> 00:18:15.169
I would underscore that.

00:18:15.169 --> 00:18:16.169
I would bold it and underline it and highlight
it for all of you today.

00:18:16.169 --> 00:18:17.169
He has not made a decision yet.

00:18:17.169 --> 00:18:18.169
I know there's a lot of rumors out there,
but let's not get ahead of where the President

00:18:18.169 --> 00:18:19.169
is on this.

00:18:19.169 --> 00:18:20.169
The second point I would make is that I would
like to be clear that any options that we're

00:18:20.169 --> 00:18:21.169
considering would be a response to the CW
attack, to the CW use, that we're not contemplating

00:18:21.169 --> 00:18:22.169
any action that would be -- for example, I
know people have asked about this in the past

00:18:22.169 --> 00:18:23.169
-- aimed at regime change.

00:18:23.169 --> 00:18:24.169
Nothing like that.

00:18:24.169 --> 00:18:25.169
It's a direct response to CW use.

00:18:25.169 --> 00:18:26.169
QUESTION: Can I ask why not, though?

00:18:26.169 --> 00:18:27.169
I mean, your stated policy goal from President
Obama two years ago, almost to the day -- it

00:18:27.169 --> 00:18:28.169
might even be the actual one-year -- two-year
anniversary but --

00:18:28.169 --> 00:18:29.169
QUESTION: It is.

00:18:29.169 --> 00:18:30.169
QUESTION: -- that President Assad should go.

00:18:30.169 --> 00:18:31.169
MS.

00:18:31.169 --> 00:18:32.169
HARF: Well --

00:18:32.169 --> 00:18:33.169
QUESTION: So if you're --

00:18:33.169 --> 00:18:34.169
MS.

00:18:34.169 --> 00:18:35.169
HARF: We continue to believe that President
Assad has lost all legitimacy and that he

00:18:35.169 --> 00:18:36.169
should go.

00:18:36.169 --> 00:18:37.169
Any specific action taken in response to the
CW attack will be -- again, in response to

00:18:37.169 --> 00:18:38.169
that attack.

00:18:38.169 --> 00:18:39.169
We will continue helping the opposition gain
strength on the ground.

00:18:39.169 --> 00:18:40.169
But I think we've made clear that there's
no military solution to this conflict, and

00:18:40.169 --> 00:18:41.169
that's exactly why we remain fully invested
in the Geneva process.

00:18:41.169 --> 00:18:43.129
QUESTION: But I mean -- in fact, I think this
was the subject of a Wall Street Journal article

00:18:43.129 --> 00:18:47.169
or op-ed today -- I mean, don't you -- if
you want to prevent the use of chemical weapons

00:18:47.169 --> 00:18:51.380
again, don't you just need to eliminate the
source of the chemical weapons?

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:52.380
MS.

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:54.150
HARF: Well, that's a very broad statement,
Elise.

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:55.549
What exactly do you mean by "the source?"

00:18:55.549 --> 00:18:59.940
QUESTION: I mean, if you're saying that the
regime used chemical weapons and you need

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.840
to respond to these chemical weapons and you
want to prevent them from happening again,

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.549
why not just get rid of Assad in your response?

00:19:06.549 --> 00:19:11.470
Why shouldn't the ultimate response be to
stop them from ever being used by this regime

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:12.470
again?

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:13.470
MS.

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:14.470
HARF: Again, the President hasn't made a decision.

00:19:14.470 --> 00:19:15.470
He will be making one based on --

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:16.519
QUESTION: So it's possible that it could be
regime change then.

00:19:16.519 --> 00:19:17.519
MS.

00:19:17.519 --> 00:19:20.879
HARF: I just said very clearly that we are
not contemplating any action aimed at regime

00:19:20.879 --> 00:19:21.879
change.

00:19:21.879 --> 00:19:22.950
QUESTION: But I don't understand why not,
though.

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:23.950
MS.

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:24.950
HARF: Because we're not.

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:27.399
QUESTION: On the -- did you say --

00:19:27.399 --> 00:19:28.399
QUESTION: We're not going to --

00:19:28.399 --> 00:19:29.399
QUESTION: -- and I -- forgive me because I
--

00:19:29.399 --> 00:19:30.399
MS.

00:19:30.399 --> 00:19:31.399
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:19:31.399 --> 00:19:33.119
QUESTION: -- did you say that it was aimed
at -- whatever the response is going to be,

00:19:33.119 --> 00:19:35.350
it is aimed at deterring future use of chemical
weapons --

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:36.350
MS.

00:19:36.350 --> 00:19:37.350
HARF: I think --

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.350
QUESTION: -- as part of the --

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:39.350
MS.

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:42.489
HARF: I think as we get closer to a response,
clearly that's one thing that people are talking

00:19:42.489 --> 00:19:43.489
about.

00:19:43.489 --> 00:19:45.409
Clearly, we want to deter future use of chemical
weapons use.

00:19:45.409 --> 00:19:46.409
QUESTION: Okay.

00:19:46.409 --> 00:19:47.409
Can I ask then --

00:19:47.409 --> 00:19:48.409
MS.

00:19:48.409 --> 00:19:49.409
HARF: So I wouldn't disagree with that notion.

00:19:49.409 --> 00:19:50.409
QUESTION: Okay.

00:19:50.409 --> 00:19:55.740
Was that also the aim of the first -- of the
response to the first time that you had documented

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.679
evidence of a chemical weapons attack?

00:19:57.679 --> 00:19:58.679
MS.

00:19:58.679 --> 00:20:02.270
HARF: I think when we announced our policy
change we said that we were increasing our

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:06.440
assistance to the opposition to help them
grow stronger on the ground in their fight

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:07.440
against the Assad regime.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:14.570
QUESTION: So why wasn't it then, after the
Assad regime had, to your understanding, shown

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:22.690
itself willing and able to use chemical weapons,
why wasn't your response to the first time

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:28.519
something that you thought would be a deterrent
to the use -- to a second or a third or a

00:20:28.519 --> 00:20:29.519
fourth use?

00:20:29.519 --> 00:20:30.519
MS.

00:20:30.519 --> 00:20:31.519
HARF: Well, I'm not saying that it wasn't,
actually.

00:20:31.519 --> 00:20:36.059
I think that, clearly, we want to take actions
that would deter the regime from using chemical

00:20:36.059 --> 00:20:37.139
weapons in the future.

00:20:37.139 --> 00:20:40.389
And what we saw here was a gross escalation
of the use of chemical weapons.

00:20:40.389 --> 00:20:41.389
QUESTION: So in other --

00:20:41.389 --> 00:20:42.389
MS.

00:20:42.389 --> 00:20:45.309
HARF: So I'm not saying that it wasn't part
of the decision that went in to increase the

00:20:45.309 --> 00:20:47.340
scale and the scope of the assistance then.

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:51.820
QUESTION: Well, if it wasn't -- or if it was,
if you're saying -- I mean, can you say for

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:56.770
sure -- can you say positively whether it
was -- whether that was one of the goals of

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:58.059
the response the first time around?

00:20:58.059 --> 00:20:59.059
MS.

00:20:59.059 --> 00:21:00.419
HARF: I think that's certainly been a goal
of ours all along.

00:21:00.419 --> 00:21:01.419
QUESTION: Okay.

00:21:01.419 --> 00:21:02.419
MS.

00:21:02.419 --> 00:21:03.419
HARF: And I would say that at about this decision
as well.

00:21:03.419 --> 00:21:04.419
Yes.

00:21:04.419 --> 00:21:05.419
QUESTION: So here's -- okay.

00:21:05.419 --> 00:21:10.239
So the -- can we assume that the range of
options that the President is considering

00:21:10.239 --> 00:21:18.830
now start above the level of increasing the
size and the scope and scale of the -- of

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:23.489
your support for the opposition, given the
fact that deterrence was one of your goals

00:21:23.489 --> 00:21:26.789
the first time around, and it clearly, clearly
didn't work?

00:21:26.789 --> 00:21:27.789
MS.

00:21:27.789 --> 00:21:32.090
HARF: I think it's fair to say that we've
been clear that this was a mass escalation

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:37.711
in chemical weapons use and that we are going
to be making a decision -- the President is

00:21:37.711 --> 00:21:38.711
-- on an appropriate response.

00:21:38.711 --> 00:21:39.711
QUESTION: All right.

00:21:39.711 --> 00:21:40.711
MS.

00:21:40.711 --> 00:21:41.711
HARF: So I think that's a fair --

00:21:41.711 --> 00:21:42.711
QUESTION: Well, would you be willing to concede
--

00:21:42.711 --> 00:21:43.711
MS.

00:21:43.711 --> 00:21:44.711
HARF: I think that's a fair judgment to make,
Matt.

00:21:44.711 --> 00:21:45.711
QUESTION: Would you be willing to concede
-- would you be willing to concede that your

00:21:45.711 --> 00:21:50.679
response to the first documented case of chemical
weapons use was not effective as a deterrent

00:21:50.679 --> 00:21:54.340
to the Assad regime using chemical weapons
again?

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:55.340
MS.

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:57.309
HARF: Well, clearly we have now assessed that
the Assad regime did use chemical weapons

00:21:57.309 --> 00:21:58.309
again.

00:21:58.309 --> 00:21:59.309
QUESTION: All right.

00:21:59.309 --> 00:22:00.570
Now, and I've just got two more and these
are very brief.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:01.570
They're on the calls.

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:02.570
MS.

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:03.570
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:04.570
QUESTION: I just want to make sure -- his
calls this morning.

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:05.570
Both of them -- anything other than Syria
discussed with Lavrov that you're aware of?

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:06.570
MS.

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:07.570
HARF: I don't know.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:08.570
I haven't gotten a full readout of that.

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:12.029
I will check on that, and if we can give you
a more fulsome readout, I --

00:22:12.029 --> 00:22:15.269
QUESTION: And the Moroccan call was about
Syria -- or Syria-centric?

00:22:15.269 --> 00:22:16.269
MS.

00:22:16.269 --> 00:22:17.269
HARF: Syria was discussed.

00:22:17.269 --> 00:22:18.269
Yes.

00:22:18.269 --> 00:22:20.710
QUESTION: Can I ask why all this talk about
a response?

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:21.710
Why not just take the response?

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:22.710
MS.

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:24.679
HARF: Well, at some point, the President will
be making a decision.

00:22:24.679 --> 00:22:25.679
QUESTION: It just seems that there's --

00:22:25.679 --> 00:22:26.679
MS.

00:22:26.679 --> 00:22:28.559
HARF: These are very serious issues, and we
need to calibrate and discuss and --

00:22:28.559 --> 00:22:29.559
QUESTION: Well, it doesn't --

00:22:29.559 --> 00:22:30.559
MS.

00:22:30.559 --> 00:22:32.440
HARF: -- debate any potential response very
carefully with the national security team.

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:34.749
QUESTION: Well, it doesn't sound as if you're
calibrating at all.

00:22:34.749 --> 00:22:38.229
I mean, all we hear is that there's going
to be a response, there's going to be a response.

00:22:38.229 --> 00:22:42.700
I mean, why do you have to keep telling us
or anybody that there's going to be a response?

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:44.729
Why don't you just warn the Syrians and make
your response?

00:22:44.729 --> 00:22:45.729
MS.

00:22:45.729 --> 00:22:48.529
HARF: Because the President hasn't made a
decision about an appropriate response yet.

00:22:48.529 --> 00:22:49.529
We -- so just to be clear --

00:22:49.529 --> 00:22:52.659
QUESTION: So it's okay to just talk about
some -- and you keep saying that you don't

00:22:52.659 --> 00:22:57.119
want to talk about some hypothetical decision
that wasn't made, but you definitely do seem

00:22:57.119 --> 00:23:00.909
to be talking about some hypothetical response
that hasn't been decided on.

00:23:00.909 --> 00:23:01.909
MS.

00:23:01.909 --> 00:23:04.340
HARF: I have been clear that we are going
-- the President is going to make a decision

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:05.340
on a response.

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:08.369
Let's remember this attack didn't take place
that long ago.

00:23:08.369 --> 00:23:14.690
We've been gathering information; we have
been working, obviously with the UN at first,

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:18.330
to see -- with the UN investigation to see
if they would be granted access.

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:19.330
They weren't.

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.940
We've been gathering information in the days
since, and we've put an assessment together.

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:24.820
This didn't happen three months ago.

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:29.529
It just happened recently, and any response
is a very -- it's an important decision that

00:23:29.529 --> 00:23:32.769
the President and the national security team
certainly don't take lightly and they don't

00:23:32.769 --> 00:23:37.309
make quickly just to comport with what people
-- with the timeline in which people think

00:23:37.309 --> 00:23:38.309
it should be made.

00:23:38.309 --> 00:23:41.249
QUESTION: I'm not saying that it should be
any specific timeline, but you definitely

00:23:41.249 --> 00:23:44.210
seem to be talking a lot about some hypothetical
response.

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:45.210
MS.

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:46.210
HARF: Yes.

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:48.770
QUESTION: So I mean, why don't you just -- I
mean, I just don't understand why all the

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:49.770
-- there's just a lot of public talk about
it.

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:50.770
MS.

00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:51.770
HARF: That's because you keep asking me questions
about our response.

00:23:51.770 --> 00:23:52.770
QUESTION: No, no.

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:53.770
In all the --

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:54.770
MS.

00:23:54.770 --> 00:23:55.770
HARF: Yes.

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:59.460
QUESTION: -- statements that the Administration
and --

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:02.899
QUESTION: Let's remember we didn't ask the
Secretary to come down yesterday and make

00:24:02.899 --> 00:24:03.899
--

00:24:03.899 --> 00:24:04.899
MS.

00:24:04.899 --> 00:24:05.899
HARF: I know.

00:24:05.899 --> 00:24:06.899
And to be clear, his --

00:24:06.899 --> 00:24:07.899
QUESTION: So --

00:24:07.899 --> 00:24:08.899
MS.

00:24:08.899 --> 00:24:09.899
HARF: -- a lot of his statement wasn't about
a response.

00:24:09.899 --> 00:24:10.899
It was about the international norms that
were violated.

00:24:10.899 --> 00:24:12.549
It was about the grotesque situation that
had happened here.

00:24:12.549 --> 00:24:17.690
It was about the Assad regime failing to stand
by what they said and give access to the UN.

00:24:17.690 --> 00:24:21.269
So his statement wasn't all about a response,
but it made clear that we will respond, absolutely.

00:24:21.269 --> 00:24:25.429
QUESTION: Marie, Marie, could you explain
to us if deterrence also includes neutralizing

00:24:25.429 --> 00:24:26.429
the capacity -- the technical capacity the
regime has to launch chemical weapons?

00:24:26.429 --> 00:24:27.429
MS.

00:24:27.429 --> 00:24:40.990
HARF: I'm not going to get into that level
of detail about what potential outcomes might

00:24:40.990 --> 00:24:41.990
come from this decision.

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:42.990
QUESTION: But you do assign the final responsibility
for launching any chemical attack with the

00:24:42.990 --> 00:24:44.149
President of Syria Bashar al-Assad, correct?

00:24:44.149 --> 00:24:45.149
MS.

00:24:45.149 --> 00:24:46.149
HARF: Correct, yes.

00:24:46.149 --> 00:24:47.149
QUESTION: Okay.

00:24:47.149 --> 00:24:48.149
So he --

00:24:48.149 --> 00:24:49.149
MS.

00:24:49.149 --> 00:24:50.149
HARF: With him and with the regime.

00:24:50.149 --> 00:24:51.149
QUESTION: He is actually an actual target,
he could be an actual target --

00:24:51.149 --> 00:24:52.149
MS.

00:24:52.149 --> 00:24:53.149
HARF: I did not say that.

00:24:53.149 --> 00:24:54.149
QUESTION: -- as part of that deterrence, correct?

00:24:54.149 --> 00:24:55.149
MS.

00:24:55.149 --> 00:24:56.149
HARF: I did not say that.

00:24:56.149 --> 00:24:58.009
I said that we are not contemplating any action
aimed at regime change.

00:24:58.009 --> 00:24:59.009
QUESTION: Okay.

00:24:59.009 --> 00:25:00.009
So you are --

00:25:00.009 --> 00:25:01.009
MS.

00:25:01.009 --> 00:25:02.009
HARF: So don't take those words --

00:25:02.009 --> 00:25:03.009
QUESTION: All right.

00:25:03.009 --> 00:25:04.009
Okay.

00:25:04.009 --> 00:25:05.009
MS.

00:25:05.009 --> 00:25:06.009
HARF: -- three steps down the road there.

00:25:06.009 --> 00:25:07.009
QUESTION: I'm trying to understand --

00:25:07.009 --> 00:25:08.009
MS.

00:25:08.009 --> 00:25:09.009
HARF: I know.

00:25:09.009 --> 00:25:10.009
QUESTION: -- to clarify --

00:25:10.009 --> 00:25:11.009
MS.

00:25:11.009 --> 00:25:12.009
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:25:12.009 --> 00:25:13.009
QUESTION: -- because he's -- at the end of
the day, he's the decision maker.

00:25:13.009 --> 00:25:14.009
He's the person that is going to continue
to launch or not to launch.

00:25:14.009 --> 00:25:15.009
So should he or should he not be a legitimate
target?

00:25:15.009 --> 00:25:16.009
MS.

00:25:16.009 --> 00:25:17.700
HARF: I'm not going to answer that question
that way, Said.

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:20.889
I have said that we are not contemplating
any action that's aimed at regime change,

00:25:20.889 --> 00:25:22.099
and I'm just not going to go any further on
that.

00:25:22.099 --> 00:25:23.099
QUESTION: Can we --

00:25:23.099 --> 00:25:24.789
QUESTION: Even though that's your stated policy
goal?

00:25:24.789 --> 00:25:26.659
I mean, that's your stated policy goal.

00:25:26.659 --> 00:25:31.239
So what you're saying is any action that you're
going to take is not in line with your stated

00:25:31.239 --> 00:25:32.769
policy goal of Assad leaving office.

00:25:32.769 --> 00:25:33.769
MS.

00:25:33.769 --> 00:25:35.460
HARF: I would strongly disagree with that,
Elise.

00:25:35.460 --> 00:25:38.840
We have been clear that our policy goal is
Assad leaving office, and that needs to happen

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:43.259
through a political solution, through a Geneva-like
process that we're working through right now.

00:25:43.259 --> 00:25:44.710
QUESTION: That has --

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:45.710
MS.

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:46.710
HARF: That is our policy goal.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.549
QUESTION: That actually no -- that I mean,
by all accounts, has absolutely made no progress

00:25:49.549 --> 00:25:51.379
since you started this whole process.

00:25:51.379 --> 00:25:53.559
I don't even know how long ago it was.

00:25:53.559 --> 00:26:01.309
I mean, what progress have you made since
Secretary Kerry and Foreign Minister Lavrov

00:26:01.309 --> 00:26:06.080
first -- since the first Geneva meeting, what
progress has been made in getting the two

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:07.080
sides to come together?

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:08.080
MS.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:09.190
HARF: Well, I think a couple of points I would
point to.

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:10.869
Clearly, we all know this is a difficult process.

00:26:10.869 --> 00:26:15.179
And again, this isn't going to happen just
overnight because it's the best thing for

00:26:15.179 --> 00:26:16.179
the Syrian people.

00:26:16.179 --> 00:26:19.979
When the Foreign Minister -- when Foreign
Minister Lavrov was here for the 2+2, he and

00:26:19.979 --> 00:26:24.120
Secretary Kerry reaffirmed their commitment
to a Geneva-like process and said they would

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:25.570
continue working together on it.

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:28.200
We've seen a couple of other things happen
in the past months.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.269
One is that the opposition has elected leaders.

00:26:30.269 --> 00:26:35.359
We saw that as a key step towards determining
representation for a Geneva convention.

00:26:35.359 --> 00:26:38.979
Obviously, we all know that representation
and attendance has been one of the outstanding

00:26:38.979 --> 00:26:40.359
issues on Geneva.

00:26:40.359 --> 00:26:44.609
And Wendy Sherman, Ambassador Ford, and others
have continued working with the UN and the

00:26:44.609 --> 00:26:46.120
Russians to make progress.

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:47.609
Clearly, progress is going to be slow.

00:26:47.609 --> 00:26:52.299
This is a very complicated situation, and
if it were easy, it would've been done months

00:26:52.299 --> 00:26:53.299
ago.

00:26:53.299 --> 00:26:54.929
But we're very committed and invested in the
process still.

00:26:54.929 --> 00:26:58.679
QUESTION: But what about getting the two sides,
the regime and the opposition, together?

00:26:58.679 --> 00:27:00.340
I mean, you're -- a lot of this --

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:01.340
MS.

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:03.046
HARF: Well, a lot goes -- that's a very simple
way of talking about Geneva.

00:27:03.046 --> 00:27:05.179
A lot goes into getting to that point.

00:27:05.179 --> 00:27:08.830
All of those little nitpicky things are being
worked through right now with us and the Russians

00:27:08.830 --> 00:27:09.830
and the UN.

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:13.549
It's not just flipping a switch and saying,
"Regime, opposition, come to Geneva for a

00:27:13.549 --> 00:27:14.549
conference."

00:27:14.549 --> 00:27:17.220
We want to have a conference at the right
time, when we feel it can be most successful,

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:20.979
because that's ultimately what's in the best
interest of the Syrian people.

00:27:20.979 --> 00:27:24.729
And we're just not there yet, but we continue
working with the Russians and the UN on it.

00:27:24.729 --> 00:27:32.539
QUESTION: Don't you think this latest undeniable
action by the regime kind of makes the likelihood

00:27:32.539 --> 00:27:38.260
of a -- of the opposition sitting down with
them highly improbable?

00:27:38.260 --> 00:27:39.260
MS.

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:40.260
HARF: Not at all.

00:27:40.260 --> 00:27:42.840
I think it actually underscores the importance
of the political process and why there is

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:44.249
no military solution here.

00:27:44.249 --> 00:27:49.779
I think it underscores why we need to work
through a political process to end this exact

00:27:49.779 --> 00:27:51.249
bloodshed in Syria.

00:27:51.249 --> 00:27:57.049
QUESTION: Is there not a danger that if you
do attack, which looks like will happen, that

00:27:57.049 --> 00:28:02.009
a political solution will be -- that there
just won't be that interest in coming to the

00:28:02.009 --> 00:28:05.159
table, that in the end it will be a military
solution?

00:28:05.159 --> 00:28:06.159
MS.

00:28:06.159 --> 00:28:09.479
HARF: Well, our position has not changed that
we don't believe there's a military solution

00:28:09.479 --> 00:28:13.350
here, that any response -- again, which I
will say, a decision has not been made on

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:17.369
-- is aimed at responding to the CW use.

00:28:17.369 --> 00:28:22.349
But our position on the ultimate necessity
of a political solution has not changed here.

00:28:22.349 --> 00:28:24.440
QUESTION: Marie, is the response -- has the
response --

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:25.440
MS.

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:26.440
HARF: I'll get to you in one second.

00:28:26.440 --> 00:28:27.440
Finish up?

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:32.649
QUESTION: Is the response that the President
is considering limited to military force?

00:28:32.649 --> 00:28:33.649
MS.

00:28:33.649 --> 00:28:35.889
HARF: He is looking at a wide range of options.

00:28:35.889 --> 00:28:38.269
I'm not going to further catalogue that for
you.

00:28:38.269 --> 00:28:42.239
I think we've been clear for a year that the
President has a variety of military contingencies

00:28:42.239 --> 00:28:44.919
on his desk, options for Syria that he can
--

00:28:44.919 --> 00:28:45.919
QUESTION: Short of --

00:28:45.919 --> 00:28:46.919
MS.

00:28:46.919 --> 00:28:50.820
HARF: -- that he can take, if he chooses to
so, but I'm not going to categorize it that

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:51.820
way.

00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:52.820
QUESTION: And then just one more.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:53.889
Could the presence --

00:28:53.889 --> 00:28:54.889
MS.

00:28:54.889 --> 00:28:55.889
HARF: Just one second.

00:28:55.889 --> 00:28:59.169
QUESTION: Could the presence of the UN team
on the ground in Damascus delay or to come

00:28:59.169 --> 00:29:04.519
into the -- be part of the deciding factor
on when to attack?

00:29:04.519 --> 00:29:05.519
MS.

00:29:05.519 --> 00:29:09.379
HARF: I don't want to even venture to guess
how that might play into some sort of hypothetical

00:29:09.379 --> 00:29:10.379
decision.

00:29:10.379 --> 00:29:11.379
QUESTION: Can I --

00:29:11.379 --> 00:29:12.379
MS.

00:29:12.379 --> 00:29:13.379
HARF: Yes.

00:29:13.379 --> 00:29:14.379
QUESTION: Short of your response to the last
question, there is one option that has been

00:29:14.379 --> 00:29:15.379
ruled out; is that correct?

00:29:15.379 --> 00:29:16.379
MS.

00:29:16.379 --> 00:29:17.379
HARF: Regime change.

00:29:17.379 --> 00:29:18.379
QUESTION: No.

00:29:18.379 --> 00:29:19.379
MS.

00:29:19.379 --> 00:29:20.379
HARF: What did you say?

00:29:20.379 --> 00:29:21.379
QUESTION: There is one option that has been
ruled out.

00:29:21.379 --> 00:29:22.379
MS.

00:29:22.379 --> 00:29:23.379
HARF: Oh, boots on the ground.

00:29:23.379 --> 00:29:24.379
QUESTION: Yes.

00:29:24.379 --> 00:29:25.379
MS.

00:29:25.379 --> 00:29:26.379
HARF: Correct.

00:29:26.379 --> 00:29:27.379
QUESTION: Right?

00:29:27.379 --> 00:29:28.379
Okay.

00:29:28.379 --> 00:29:29.379
So you're not --

00:29:29.379 --> 00:29:30.379
QUESTION: And a no-fly zone?

00:29:30.379 --> 00:29:31.379
QUESTION: So I can't read my notes, I apologize,
so I want to ask -- so I have to ask you instead.

00:29:31.379 --> 00:29:32.379
MS.

00:29:32.379 --> 00:29:33.379
HARF: That often happens to me, too.

00:29:33.379 --> 00:29:34.379
I sympathize with you.

00:29:34.379 --> 00:29:35.379
QUESTION: Did you welcome -- did you say you
welcome the Arab League decision?

00:29:35.379 --> 00:29:36.379
Is that correct?

00:29:36.379 --> 00:29:37.379
MS.

00:29:37.379 --> 00:29:38.379
HARF: The statement?

00:29:38.379 --> 00:29:39.379
QUESTION: Yeah, the statement.

00:29:39.379 --> 00:29:40.379
I'm sorry, the statement, yes.

00:29:40.379 --> 00:29:41.379
MS.

00:29:41.379 --> 00:29:42.379
HARF: I -- yeah, I'm fine saying that we welcome
the statement.

00:29:42.379 --> 00:29:43.379
QUESTION: Yes.

00:29:43.379 --> 00:29:44.379
Okay, all right.

00:29:44.379 --> 00:29:45.379
So if you're welcoming that, do you -- are
you at all disappointed by the responses by

00:29:45.379 --> 00:29:48.859
the Russians and the Chinese so far, or non-responses
to this -- to -- if you say that you'd like

00:29:48.859 --> 00:29:51.710
-- you've encouraged people to make strong
statements condemning this and attacking it.

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:52.710
Are you disappointed?

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:53.710
Do you think that, as has been said numerous
times, even in -- by the previous Secretary

00:29:53.710 --> 00:29:54.710
of State, that they're on the wrong side of
history --

00:29:54.710 --> 00:29:55.710
MS.

00:29:55.710 --> 00:29:56.710
HARF: I think --

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:57.710
QUESTION: -- by ignoring --

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:58.710
MS.

00:29:58.710 --> 00:29:59.710
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:00.710
QUESTION: -- or choosing to not come out and
condemn what happened?

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:01.710
MS.

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:02.710
HARF: Well, I actually -- I would reference
what the Secretary said yesterday, that anybody

00:30:02.710 --> 00:30:03.710
who perpetrates this notion or this falsehood
that anybody but the regime could be behind

00:30:03.710 --> 00:30:04.710
this needs to check their conscience.

00:30:04.710 --> 00:30:05.710
QUESTION: Right.

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:06.710
But I want you -- I would like him --

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:07.710
MS.

00:30:07.710 --> 00:30:08.710
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:30:08.710 --> 00:30:09.710
QUESTION: -- but since he's not here and you
are --

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:10.710
MS.

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:11.710
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:29.809
QUESTION: -- to identify who it is that is
-- that needs to check their conscience.

00:30:29.809 --> 00:30:30.809
MS.

00:30:30.809 --> 00:30:37.359
HARF: I'm not going to parse that further
for you.

00:30:37.359 --> 00:30:38.359
I will --

00:30:38.359 --> 00:30:39.359
QUESTION: No --

00:30:39.359 --> 00:30:40.359
MS.

00:30:40.359 --> 00:30:41.359
HARF: I'm just not going to do it, Matt.

00:30:41.359 --> 00:30:43.210
I will say that we have -- it's been clear
who's been out there saying -- casting doubt

00:30:43.210 --> 00:30:47.139
on the fact that the regime used these chemical
weapons.

00:30:47.139 --> 00:30:51.909
We've also been clear that even though we
have differences of opinion with some of our

00:30:51.909 --> 00:30:56.330
partners around the world, including the Russians,
on Syria, that we will continue talking to

00:30:56.330 --> 00:30:58.419
them about it, which is clear from the Secretary's
call today.

00:30:58.419 --> 00:30:59.419
QUESTION: All right.

00:30:59.419 --> 00:31:02.840
And the last thing is that when you say that
your goal is not regime change --

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:03.840
MS.

00:31:03.840 --> 00:31:04.840
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:05.840
QUESTION: -- but you also say that Assad --

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:06.840
MS.

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:09.239
HARF: That we're not contemplating any action
aimed at regime change, correct, in this response.

00:31:09.239 --> 00:31:10.369
QUESTION: Right, okay.

00:31:10.369 --> 00:31:13.019
I want to divorce it from this -- the response
to the chemical weapons.

00:31:13.019 --> 00:31:14.019
MS.

00:31:14.019 --> 00:31:15.019
HARF: Okay.

00:31:15.019 --> 00:31:17.789
QUESTION: Your goal, though, your ultimate
goal is not regime change.

00:31:17.789 --> 00:31:18.789
Correct?

00:31:18.789 --> 00:31:19.789
MS.

00:31:19.789 --> 00:31:22.479
HARF: Well, we've been clear that our ultimate
-- I mean, policy goal, our ultimate position

00:31:22.479 --> 00:31:24.159
is that Assad no longer has legitimacy --

00:31:24.159 --> 00:31:25.159
QUESTION: Does that mean --

00:31:25.159 --> 00:31:26.159
MS.

00:31:26.159 --> 00:31:27.159
HARF: -- and must not be in power.

00:31:27.159 --> 00:31:28.159
Correct.

00:31:28.159 --> 00:31:29.159
QUESTION: Right.

00:31:29.159 --> 00:31:33.570
But does that mean that anyone whose last
name is Assad who is in this family cannot

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:34.919
be in a leadership position?

00:31:34.919 --> 00:31:36.549
Like his wife, for example, or his uncle,
or his brother.

00:31:36.549 --> 00:31:37.549
Because --

00:31:37.549 --> 00:31:38.549
MS.

00:31:38.549 --> 00:31:41.169
HARF: I'm not going to make that broad statement
or generalize in that way at this point.

00:31:41.169 --> 00:31:42.169
QUESTION: Okay.

00:31:42.169 --> 00:31:48.779
So then it really isn't -- your goal really
isn't an end to the Assad regime necessarily,

00:31:48.779 --> 00:31:52.039
because someone else in that family, you think,
could be --

00:31:52.039 --> 00:31:53.039
MS.

00:31:53.039 --> 00:31:55.799
HARF: I'm not making a comment on that one
way or the other.

00:31:55.799 --> 00:31:56.799
I'm just not.

00:31:56.799 --> 00:32:01.590
QUESTION: So you're not looking for the end
of the Assad family's tyranny over Syria here

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:02.590
--

00:32:02.590 --> 00:32:03.590
MS.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:04.590
HARF: We've been clear that the Assad regime
--

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:05.590
QUESTION: -- just Bashar Assad.

00:32:05.590 --> 00:32:06.590
MS.

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:10.509
HARF: We have been clear that the Assad regime
-- excuse me -- can no longer be in power.

00:32:10.509 --> 00:32:11.509
It has lost all legitimacy.

00:32:11.509 --> 00:32:12.509
QUESTION: The Assad regime.

00:32:12.509 --> 00:32:13.509
MS.

00:32:13.509 --> 00:32:14.509
HARF: I'm not going to further parse it for
you than that.

00:32:14.509 --> 00:32:15.509
I'm just not.

00:32:15.509 --> 00:32:16.509
QUESTION: All right.

00:32:16.509 --> 00:32:17.509
QUESTION: (Inaudible.)

00:32:17.509 --> 00:32:18.509
MS.

00:32:18.509 --> 00:32:19.509
HARF: Yes.

00:32:19.509 --> 00:32:20.509
QUESTION: You were speaking about the political
solution --

00:32:20.509 --> 00:32:21.509
MS.

00:32:21.509 --> 00:32:22.509
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:32:22.509 --> 00:32:23.509
QUESTION: -- and the Geneva 2 conference.

00:32:23.509 --> 00:32:27.029
As -- I'm sure you're aware that UN Under-Secretary-General
Jeff Feltman is in Tehran.

00:32:27.029 --> 00:32:35.769
A trip like this is not very ordinary for
a -- such a high-level UN official, especially

00:32:35.769 --> 00:32:40.989
when he's an American, and it could not have
happened without consultation with the U.S.

00:32:40.989 --> 00:32:41.989
Administration.

00:32:41.989 --> 00:32:43.830
What can you tell us about the purpose behind
that trip?

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:44.830
MS.

00:32:44.830 --> 00:32:46.369
HARF: Well, I actually don't have anything
for you on that.

00:32:46.369 --> 00:32:49.419
I would refer you to the UN, as he's not a
U.S. official.

00:32:49.419 --> 00:32:52.710
If I have any more details, I'm happy to share
them, but again, he's not there representing

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:56.289
the United States, and I don't know if I would
actually make the assumption that you just

00:32:56.289 --> 00:32:57.289
made.

00:32:57.289 --> 00:32:58.289
I just don't have the facts about his trip.

00:32:58.289 --> 00:33:00.559
QUESTION: Can we assume that the response
will be military at least?

00:33:00.559 --> 00:33:01.559
MS.

00:33:01.559 --> 00:33:04.489
HARF: I would not make that assumption.

00:33:04.489 --> 00:33:07.679
The President is reviewing a number of options
and has not made a decision yet.

00:33:07.679 --> 00:33:13.809
QUESTION: So the Syrian Government will be
held accountable by the U.S. through a nonmilitary

00:33:13.809 --> 00:33:14.809
response?

00:33:14.809 --> 00:33:15.809
MS.

00:33:15.809 --> 00:33:16.839
HARF: Again, there -- he has a range of options,
and he hasn't made a decision yet.

00:33:16.839 --> 00:33:18.070
QUESTION: What are the options?

00:33:18.070 --> 00:33:19.070
What are the --

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:20.070
MS.

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:21.070
HARF: There's a range of them.

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:22.070
We've been clear that some of those include
military contingencies.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:23.070
Some don't.

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:24.070
I'm not going to --

00:33:24.070 --> 00:33:25.711
QUESTION: What are the ones that don't include
military --

00:33:25.711 --> 00:33:26.711
MS.

00:33:26.711 --> 00:33:27.711
HARF: I'm not going to further parse it for
you.

00:33:27.711 --> 00:33:28.919
They're on his desk and he'll make a decision
when he's ready.

00:33:28.919 --> 00:33:29.919
QUESTION: Marie, you mentioned --

00:33:29.919 --> 00:33:30.919
MS.

00:33:30.919 --> 00:33:31.919
HARF: Yes.

00:33:31.919 --> 00:33:32.919
QUESTION: -- a classified briefing that would
be given to Congress.

00:33:32.919 --> 00:33:33.919
MS.

00:33:33.919 --> 00:33:34.919
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:33:34.919 --> 00:33:35.919
QUESTION: Do you have any more information
on who will be making that briefing?

00:33:35.919 --> 00:33:36.919
MS.

00:33:36.919 --> 00:33:37.919
HARF: I don't.

00:33:37.919 --> 00:33:38.919
QUESTION: (Inaudible.)

00:33:38.919 --> 00:33:39.919
MS.

00:33:39.919 --> 00:33:40.919
HARF: If we have more information to share,
we can.

00:33:40.919 --> 00:33:41.919
I just don't have any more information for
you right now.

00:33:41.919 --> 00:33:42.919
QUESTION: And then I was -- I also wanted
to ask: I know you said you're not going to

00:33:42.919 --> 00:33:45.179
do any legal analysis on the President's forthcoming
decision, but leaving --

00:33:45.179 --> 00:33:46.179
MS.

00:33:46.179 --> 00:33:47.179
HARF: But you're going to get me to try to
do it.

00:33:47.179 --> 00:33:51.860
QUESTION: -- no, no -- just leaving that aside
and just focusing exclusively on the Assad

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:52.950
regime's use of chemical weapons --

00:33:52.950 --> 00:33:53.950
MS.

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:54.950
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:33:54.950 --> 00:33:56.309
QUESTION: -- does the Administration view
that as a violation of international law?

00:33:56.309 --> 00:33:57.309
MS.

00:33:57.309 --> 00:33:58.419
HARF: I think we were very clear.

00:33:58.419 --> 00:34:00.289
The Secretary used the word "international
norms."

00:34:00.289 --> 00:34:02.400
And actually, let me take through some of
the history on that.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:07.070
I think it might be helpful as we talk about
this issue, so thank you for the question.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:10.669
There's clearly a long history to these international
norms that goes back many years.

00:34:10.669 --> 00:34:14.470
And this isn't a comprehensive list, but I'll
just make a few points.

00:34:14.470 --> 00:34:18.880
We're going back after World War I, the use
-- in which the use of chemical weapons resulted

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:20.360
in nearly 100,000 deaths.

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:25.159
The Geneva Protocol was signed in 1925 and
ratified by 137 states.

00:34:25.159 --> 00:34:28.350
The protocol prohibited the use of chemical
weapons, quote, "in war."

00:34:28.350 --> 00:34:33.040
In 1997, the Chemical Weapons Convention entered
into force.

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:37.750
This multilateral disarmament agreement provides
for the elimination of an entire category

00:34:37.750 --> 00:34:42.890
of weapons of mass destruction under universally
applied international control and prohibits

00:34:42.890 --> 00:34:44.169
the use of chemical weapons.

00:34:44.169 --> 00:34:50.850
Currently, 189 nations, which represent about
98 percent of the global population, have

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:52.930
joined the Chemical Weapons Convention.

00:34:52.930 --> 00:34:57.330
So just a little history on the international
norms here at work.

00:34:57.330 --> 00:34:58.330
QUESTION: And is it --

00:34:58.330 --> 00:34:59.330
MS.

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:00.330
HARF: Just one second.

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:01.330
QUESTION: -- correct that Syria's --

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:02.330
QUESTION: Can you put that out?

00:35:02.330 --> 00:35:03.330
MS.

00:35:03.330 --> 00:35:04.330
HARF: Just one second.

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:05.330
QUESTION: Is it correct that Syria is not
a signatory to that convention?

00:35:05.330 --> 00:35:06.330
MS.

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:07.330
HARF: So, you're correct.

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:08.330
There are only five countries in the world
that have neither signed nor acceded to the

00:35:08.330 --> 00:35:12.030
convention, but clearly that should not enable
them to escape responsibility for their actions.

00:35:12.030 --> 00:35:16.410
There is a reason that the overwhelming majority
of the international community -- again, that

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:20.480
agrees on little else -- has stood against
the use of these weapons, and Syria should

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:24.250
not be able to flout the clearly expressed
view of the international community here.

00:35:24.250 --> 00:35:25.250
QUESTION: Who are they?

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:26.250
Who are the five?

00:35:26.250 --> 00:35:27.250
QUESTION: Can you put that list of precedents
out?

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:28.250
MS.

00:35:28.250 --> 00:35:29.250
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:30.250
Yeah.

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:31.250
We'll send something to the bullpen after
the briefing.

00:35:31.250 --> 00:35:32.250
QUESTION: Who are the five?

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:33.250
MS.

00:35:33.250 --> 00:35:34.250
HARF: And there's a little more detail, too.

00:35:34.250 --> 00:35:35.250
Who are the five --

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:36.250
QUESTION: Right.

00:35:36.250 --> 00:35:37.250
Please.

00:35:37.250 --> 00:35:38.250
Thank you.

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:39.250
MS.

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:40.590
HARF: -- that have not -- neither signed nor
acceded to the CWC: Angola, Egypt, North Korea,

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:42.480
South Sudan, and Syria.

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:43.480
Yeah.

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:44.480
Still on Syria?

00:35:44.480 --> 00:35:45.480
QUESTION: Yes.

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:46.480
MS.

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:47.480
HARF: Okay.

00:35:47.480 --> 00:35:49.610
QUESTION: Any discussion with authorities
in Athens about the possible use of U.S. bases

00:35:49.610 --> 00:35:53.310
in Greece on any potential military strike
against Syria?

00:35:53.310 --> 00:35:54.310
MS.

00:35:54.310 --> 00:35:57.840
HARF: I don't have any additional details
for you about conversations about potential

00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:00.180
options that might be part of a response.

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:02.670
QUESTION: Since you brought up the history,
I'm just wondering --

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:03.670
MS.

00:36:03.670 --> 00:36:04.670
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:08.561
QUESTION: -- if you're -- have you looked
back and found when any previous Administration

00:36:08.561 --> 00:36:16.260
has used the use of chemical weapons as a
legal justification for military or any other

00:36:16.260 --> 00:36:17.260
intervention?

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:18.260
MS.

00:36:18.260 --> 00:36:21.140
HARF: I think that I would strongly discourage
people from comparing this situation to any

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:24.200
historical other situation where chemical
weapons have been discussed.

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:25.450
QUESTION: But you're the one that just went
back to World War I.

00:36:25.450 --> 00:36:26.450
MS.

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:30.440
HARF: No, I'm talking -- I'm laying out the
historical set of international norms that

00:36:30.440 --> 00:36:31.440
have formed.

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:33.520
You're trying to compare it to being used
as a justification --

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:34.520
QUESTION: No, I'm not.

00:36:34.520 --> 00:36:35.520
No, no, no.

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:36.520
MS.

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:37.520
HARF: -- for another conflict.

00:36:37.520 --> 00:36:38.520
QUESTION: I'm not trying to compare it.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:43.080
I'm just asking you if you are aware of any
previous Administration relying on the use

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:44.670
of chemical weapons --

00:36:44.670 --> 00:36:45.670
MS.

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:46.670
HARF: Yes.

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:48.620
QUESTION: -- as justification for a military
or nonmilitary intervention.

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:53.220
I'm just wondering if -- you brought it -- you
opened the door to this, going back to World

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:54.220
War I.

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:55.220
MS.

00:36:55.220 --> 00:36:56.220
HARF: I was -- two points.

00:36:56.220 --> 00:36:59.050
I was laying out the historical international
norm against the use of chemical weapons.

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:00.050
Clearly, we're --

00:37:00.050 --> 00:37:01.050
QUESTION: Okay.

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:02.050
MS.

00:37:02.050 --> 00:37:04.130
HARF: -- all familiar with some of the history,
including recent history on this issue.

00:37:04.130 --> 00:37:07.640
And I'm not going to go into a further -- historical
analysis than that.

00:37:07.640 --> 00:37:08.640
QUESTION: But then -- don't -- okay.

00:37:08.640 --> 00:37:09.640
MS.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:10.640
HARF: Where's your next --

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:11.640
QUESTION: If you've then -- do you --

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:12.640
MS.

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:15.220
HARF: I think it's important to lay out the
historical basis and international norms against

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:16.220
the use of chemical weapons.

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:17.220
QUESTION: Then (inaudible) see what -- what
do you consider --

00:37:17.220 --> 00:37:18.220
QUESTION: You can't just --

00:37:18.220 --> 00:37:19.220
QUESTION: Respectfully, you can't just throw
it out there and then say, I'm not going to

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:20.220
answer any more questions about it.

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:21.220
MS.

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:22.220
HARF: Okay.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:23.220
So what's your next question?

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:24.220
Where were you going with that, Matt?

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:26.360
QUESTION: Well, I just wanted -- I'm asking
you a very simple question.

00:37:26.360 --> 00:37:27.360
MS.

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:28.360
HARF: Yes.

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:29.360
The answer is yes.

00:37:29.360 --> 00:37:30.360
Question?

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:31.360
QUESTION: Okay.

00:37:31.360 --> 00:37:32.360
The answer is, yes, the --

00:37:32.360 --> 00:37:33.360
MS.

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:34.360
HARF: Yes.

00:37:34.360 --> 00:37:35.460
QUESTION: -- previous administrations have
used the use of chemical weapons as the legal

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:39.240
justification for intervention, military or
otherwise, in the past.

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:40.240
They have?

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:41.240
MS.

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:43.660
HARF: I don't know about legal justification,
but I know it's been a topic of discussion

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:44.850
in past conflicts, yes.

00:37:44.850 --> 00:37:45.850
QUESTION: So you --

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:46.850
MS.

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:47.850
HARF: Hold on.

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:48.850
QUESTION: And -- okay, wait, wait.

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:49.850
Can you -- which ones?

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:50.850
MS.

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:51.850
HARF: See, I'm not going to go down this rabbit
hole with you.

00:37:51.850 --> 00:37:56.560
We are not comparing this specific case to
any other time we've concluded that a regime

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:59.350
may or may not, or whatever the discussion
was about chemical weapons.

00:37:59.350 --> 00:38:00.350
QUESTION: You --

00:38:00.350 --> 00:38:01.350
MS.

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:02.810
HARF: I laid out historical basis and international
norms --

00:38:02.810 --> 00:38:03.810
QUESTION: I know.

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:04.810
But you're so --

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:05.810
MS.

00:38:05.810 --> 00:38:06.810
HARF: -- against the use of chemical weapons.

00:38:06.810 --> 00:38:07.810
QUESTION: Can I just --

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:08.810
MS.

00:38:08.810 --> 00:38:09.810
HARF: Those are actually two different things.

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:10.810
And you're trying to take me down a rabbit
hole here.

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:11.810
QUESTION: No, I'm not.

00:38:11.810 --> 00:38:12.810
You're assuming that I'm trying to trick you
again, and I'm not.

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:13.810
I'm just asking --

00:38:13.810 --> 00:38:14.810
MS.

00:38:14.810 --> 00:38:15.810
HARF: Again, you so frequently do, it's hard
to tell when you're not.

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:16.810
(Laughter.)

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:19.920
QUESTION: It's a very straightforward question:
Are you familiar with any case in which a

00:38:19.920 --> 00:38:25.640
previous Administration has used this as the
rationale for intervention, military or otherwise?

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:26.640
MS.

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:28.820
HARF: Clearly, we're all familiar with a lot
of the history, Matt, yes.

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:29.820
QUESTION: Let me --

00:38:29.820 --> 00:38:30.820
QUESTION: But you are?

00:38:30.820 --> 00:38:31.820
QUESTION: Wait, wait, wait.

00:38:31.820 --> 00:38:32.820
Do you --

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:33.820
QUESTION: Then I'm just asking you: When?

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:34.820
Because I'm not.

00:38:34.820 --> 00:38:35.820
MS.

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:39.560
HARF: When we've discussed chemical weapons
in terms of military intervention in places

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:40.560
in the past?

00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:41.560
QUESTION: Yeah.

00:38:41.560 --> 00:38:46.070
Where has the U.S. intervened on the basis
of another country using chemical weapons?

00:38:46.070 --> 00:38:48.230
I can't think of one off the top of my head.

00:38:48.230 --> 00:38:49.230
MS.

00:38:49.230 --> 00:38:50.230
HARF: Okay.

00:38:50.230 --> 00:38:51.230
Then we'll move on.

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:52.230
QUESTION: I'm just -- but you said --

00:38:52.230 --> 00:38:53.230
QUESTION: Do you think --

00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:54.230
QUESTION: No.

00:38:54.230 --> 00:38:55.230
I'm asking -- you said yes, it has happened.

00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:56.230
So I want to know where.

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:57.230
MS.

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:58.230
HARF: I said that there have been conversations
in the past.

00:38:58.230 --> 00:39:00.520
We all lived through the past 12 years, and
we all remember the discussions about WMD

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:01.520
in the past.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:02.660
That's where I thought you were going with
that.

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:03.660
QUESTION: No, no.

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:04.660
MS.

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:05.660
HARF: I'm not going to compare it.

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:07.150
QUESTION: You see, that's why -- I'm not trying
to get you to compare this to Iraq or anything

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:08.150
else.

00:39:08.150 --> 00:39:09.150
MS.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:10.150
HARF: Okay.

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:11.480
QUESTION: I'm just trying to find out if you're
aware of any time that an administration --

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:12.480
MS.

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:13.480
HARF: Again, so then my previous statement
stands.

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:17.070
I don't want to compare this to any previous
administration, to any previous decision.

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:18.890
QUESTION: But you said -- but your answer
to my question was yes, it has happened.

00:39:18.890 --> 00:39:19.890
So I just want to --

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:20.890
MS.

00:39:20.890 --> 00:39:22.150
HARF: I'm not comparing it to any previous
administration or previous decision.

00:39:22.150 --> 00:39:23.150
QUESTION: I'm not --

00:39:23.150 --> 00:39:24.150
MS.

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:25.150
HARF: So I'm not going to have a discussion
of previous administration's decisions.

00:39:25.150 --> 00:39:26.150
QUESTION: All right.

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:27.150
Let's -- can we continue this afterwards?

00:39:27.150 --> 00:39:28.150
QUESTION: You said that -- you talk here about
international --

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:29.150
MS.

00:39:29.150 --> 00:39:30.150
HARF: No, I just want to be clear on this.

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:31.150
I'm not going to compare this to any previous
administration.

00:39:31.150 --> 00:39:32.150
QUESTION: I'm not asking for a comparison.

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:33.150
I'm just --

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:34.150
MS.

00:39:34.150 --> 00:39:35.150
HARF: Then why did you ask about previous
administrations?

00:39:35.150 --> 00:39:37.220
QUESTION: I'm wondering if any -- if you are
aware that any -- if any previous administration

00:39:37.220 --> 00:39:41.620
has used the use of chemical weapons as a
--

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:42.620
MS.

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:43.620
HARF: I don't think it's relevant in this
case.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:44.670
That's my answer and we're moving on.

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:45.670
Thank you.

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:46.670
QUESTION: You talk about --

00:39:46.670 --> 00:39:47.670
MS.

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:48.670
HARF: Said.

00:39:48.670 --> 00:39:49.670
Go ahead, Said.

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:50.670
I'll go to you next, Elise.

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:51.670
QUESTION: I just wanted to ask you, it seems
that you have concluded --

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:52.670
MS.

00:39:52.670 --> 00:39:53.670
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:39:53.670 --> 00:39:58.050
QUESTION: -- early on, on Wednesday actually,
right after the attack, that the regime was

00:39:58.050 --> 00:39:59.050
responsible for this attack.

00:39:59.050 --> 00:40:00.820
Could you explain to us -- I know you talked
about this before -- why did then the Secretary

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:02.530
took -- take the initiative and talk to Muallim?

00:40:02.530 --> 00:40:03.530
MS.

00:40:03.530 --> 00:40:04.530
HARF: Why did he?

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:05.530
QUESTION: Yeah.

00:40:05.530 --> 00:40:06.530
Why did he?

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:10.310
I mean, if you had already concluded that
the regime stands behind it, and the fact

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:14.080
that he called him to tell him that if you
had nothing to hide --

00:40:14.080 --> 00:40:15.080
MS.

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:16.080
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:17.820
QUESTION: -- you should have allowed people
in right away, which means that you know --

00:40:17.820 --> 00:40:18.820
MS.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:19.820
HARF: He called him right away.

00:40:19.820 --> 00:40:20.820
Yeah.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:21.820
QUESTION: Why did he call him?

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:22.820
Why --

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:23.820
MS.

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:24.820
HARF: He made a direct demand to the Syrian
regime that if, as they have stated, they

00:40:24.820 --> 00:40:25.900
were not responsible for these attacks, they
should allow investigators in.

00:40:25.900 --> 00:40:29.820
He gave them an opportunity to prove to the
world what they were saying publicly, and

00:40:29.820 --> 00:40:30.820
they failed to take it.

00:40:30.820 --> 00:40:31.820
QUESTION: So --

00:40:31.820 --> 00:40:32.820
MS.

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:33.820
HARF: But it's important to make a direct
demand of them.

00:40:33.820 --> 00:40:37.559
QUESTION: So in terms of just the sequence
of events, when do you -- when did you conclude

00:40:37.559 --> 00:40:42.560
that the Syrians were behind this and they
did not allow basically to sort of destroy

00:40:42.560 --> 00:40:43.560
evidence and so on?

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:44.560
MS.

00:40:44.560 --> 00:40:45.560
HARF: Well, I think from very early on there
was --

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:46.560
QUESTION: Like by Saturday, by Friday?

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:47.560
MS.

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:48.560
HARF: Right.

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:49.560
So I'll make a few points.

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:52.240
The first is the intelligence community will
be sharing more details about their assessment.

00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:56.830
But broadly speaking, there's been a preponderance
of publicly available information from the

00:40:56.830 --> 00:41:02.190
very beginning, quite frankly, that indicated
there was a mass-scale chemical weapons attack.

00:41:02.190 --> 00:41:06.320
We've known and assessed for a long time that
the Syrian regime had the capabilities to

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:08.380
do this and the opposition didn't.

00:41:08.380 --> 00:41:12.120
So obviously, we thought it was important
to investigate, to do our own assessment.

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:17.840
But this is just common sense at the heart
of it, backed up by facts and assessments

00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:19.760
and information and intelligence.

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:20.760
QUESTION: If you --

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:21.760
MS.

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:22.760
HARF: Elise.

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:23.760
QUESTION: A couple of things.

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:24.760
MS.

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:25.760
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:41:25.760 --> 00:41:26.760
QUESTION: First of all, just in response to
this just question right here --

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:27.760
MS.

00:41:27.760 --> 00:41:28.760
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:41:28.760 --> 00:41:30.640
QUESTION: -- if you knew that the regime had
all these chemical weapons --

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:31.640
MS.

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:32.640
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:41:32.640 --> 00:41:33.640
QUESTION: -- and you were afraid that they
were going to use them, and they used them

00:41:33.640 --> 00:41:38.020
before, and you warned them against crossing
a redline and they crossed it, then why -- what

00:41:38.020 --> 00:41:40.310
actions did you take to prevent further use?

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:41.310
MS.

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:42.860
HARF: In what regard?

00:41:42.860 --> 00:41:45.560
QUESTION: Further use from the regime from
using them again.

00:41:45.560 --> 00:41:46.560
Some warning?

00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:47.560
MS.

00:41:47.560 --> 00:41:48.600
HARF: Well, (a) this was a massive escalation
in use.

00:41:48.600 --> 00:41:52.330
We had not seen this level of use from the
regime in the past.

00:41:52.330 --> 00:41:56.480
I think that we made clear very strongly the
last time we assessed that they had used them

00:41:56.480 --> 00:42:00.810
in small-scale incidents that they had crossed
a redline and that we were taking action.

00:42:00.810 --> 00:42:05.500
But again, the Syrian regime has continued
at every step to take the actions that are

00:42:05.500 --> 00:42:09.470
most hurtful to their people, and that's why
the President is weighing an appropriate response

00:42:09.470 --> 00:42:10.470
in this instance.

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:12.970
QUESTION: Now you talk about international
norms and precedent --

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:13.970
MS.

00:42:13.970 --> 00:42:14.970
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:15.970
QUESTION: -- for acting in (inaudible).

00:42:15.970 --> 00:42:21.380
Do you feel that precedent on international
norms is enough, or do you feel that any response

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:23.880
would require a basis of international law?

00:42:23.880 --> 00:42:24.880
MS.

00:42:24.880 --> 00:42:29.280
HARF: I'm not going to do any further hypothetical
legal analysis than I already have here before

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:30.630
the President's made a decision.

00:42:30.630 --> 00:42:37.060
QUESTION: Can I just -- my question really
was: Are you aware of precedent for intervention

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:41.200
based on the use of chemical weapons?

00:42:41.200 --> 00:42:42.330
That's all I was asking.

00:42:42.330 --> 00:42:43.330
QUESTION: You asked that already.

00:42:43.330 --> 00:42:44.330
(Laughter.)

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:45.330
QUESTION: That's -- I just want you to understand.

00:42:45.330 --> 00:42:46.330
I'm not trying to compare.

00:42:46.330 --> 00:42:47.330
MS.

00:42:47.330 --> 00:42:48.330
HARF: I think you asked it a little differently
--

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:49.330
QUESTION: I am not --

00:42:49.330 --> 00:42:50.330
MS.

00:42:50.330 --> 00:42:51.330
HARF: -- so let me take that question and
see if I can get you a more fulsome answer.

00:42:51.330 --> 00:42:52.330
QUESTION: That's what I was trying to get
to.

00:42:52.330 --> 00:42:53.330
MS.

00:42:53.330 --> 00:42:54.330
HARF: Okay.

00:42:54.330 --> 00:42:55.330
QUESTION: I wasn't trying to go after Iraq.

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:56.330
MS.

00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:57.330
HARF: I think you asked it a little differently,
which is why I responded the way I did, so

00:42:57.330 --> 00:42:58.330
let me look into that question and see if
there's more I can share with you.

00:42:58.330 --> 00:43:01.220
QUESTION: Because I'm not -- I'm honestly
not aware of one.

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:02.450
There may very well be one.

00:43:02.450 --> 00:43:03.650
I'm just curious if you know what it is.

00:43:03.650 --> 00:43:04.650
MS.

00:43:04.650 --> 00:43:06.390
HARF: A specific response to a specific use
of chemical weapons.

00:43:06.390 --> 00:43:09.210
I think the way you asked it -- and maybe
this is why I was confused -- was whether

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:12.880
it had been part of the case broadly speaking
for military action in the past.

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:13.880
QUESTION: That's also --

00:43:13.880 --> 00:43:14.880
MS.

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:15.880
HARF: Those are two different questions, I
think.

00:43:15.880 --> 00:43:16.880
I will take it and get you --

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:17.880
QUESTION: That's -- I think it's the same
thing, but --

00:43:17.880 --> 00:43:18.880
MS.

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:20.800
HARF: They're actually two different questions,
but I will take your actual question and endeavor

00:43:20.800 --> 00:43:22.460
to get you a more fulsome response.

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:23.460
QUESTION: All right.

00:43:23.460 --> 00:43:24.460
MS.

00:43:24.460 --> 00:43:25.460
HARF: Yes.

00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:31.240
QUESTION: Marie, to be perfectly clear, yesterday
you said the U.S. has very little doubt on

00:43:31.240 --> 00:43:34.250
the fact that the regime is responsible for
the Wednesday attack.

00:43:34.250 --> 00:43:39.830
Today you have absolutely no doubt that Damascus
is behind the attack last week?

00:43:39.830 --> 00:43:40.830
MS.

00:43:40.830 --> 00:43:41.830
HARF: Correct.

00:43:41.830 --> 00:43:42.830
Correct.

00:43:42.830 --> 00:43:43.830
QUESTION: Okay.

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:46.170
And the French President has just said that
the decision from the U.S. will be taken in

00:43:46.170 --> 00:43:47.170
the coming days.

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:48.530
Is he correct?

00:43:48.530 --> 00:43:49.530
MS.

00:43:49.530 --> 00:43:53.240
HARF: Well, I think we've been clear that
we feel a sense of urgency here to make a

00:43:53.240 --> 00:43:54.240
decision.

00:43:54.240 --> 00:43:55.740
But the President hasn't made one yet.

00:43:55.740 --> 00:43:57.990
I don't want to put any timeline on it beyond
that.

00:43:57.990 --> 00:43:59.020
QUESTION: Change topic?

00:43:59.020 --> 00:44:00.020
MS.

00:44:00.020 --> 00:44:01.070
HARF: Anything else on Syria?

00:44:01.070 --> 00:44:02.070
Yes.

00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:03.070
QUESTION: I have one more.

00:44:03.070 --> 00:44:04.070
MS.

00:44:04.070 --> 00:44:05.070
HARF: Okay.

00:44:05.070 --> 00:44:07.200
QUESTION: You said, kind of following on this,
that now we're crystal clear and the intel

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:08.670
community will be providing --

00:44:08.670 --> 00:44:09.670
MS.

00:44:09.670 --> 00:44:17.890
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:44:17.890 --> 00:44:45.180
QUESTION: -- additional info within the week.

00:44:45.180 --> 00:44:59.430
My question is: What is the intelligence community
doing between Secretary Kerry's statement,

00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:00.931
which was strong and forceful and I would
assume has the backing of the intelligence

00:45:00.931 --> 00:45:02.550
community, what you're saying today, and this
delay it would seem for the report to come

00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:03.550
out?

00:45:03.550 --> 00:45:04.550
MS.

00:45:04.550 --> 00:45:05.550
HARF: Well, I wouldn't call it a delay.

00:45:05.550 --> 00:45:06.550
Clearly there's a process to getting classified
assessments to the Hill, there's a process

00:45:06.550 --> 00:45:07.550
to putting together the unclassified version
that will go publicly.

00:45:07.550 --> 00:45:08.550
I wouldn't call it a delay.

00:45:08.550 --> 00:45:09.550
There's just a process that's ongoing.

00:45:09.550 --> 00:45:10.550
QUESTION: So this is dotting Ts, crossing
Is, type of thing?

00:45:10.550 --> 00:45:11.550
There's not new information coming in since
Monday?

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:12.550
MS.

00:45:12.550 --> 00:45:13.550
HARF: It's -- I think there's new information
coming in all the time, which is what happens,

00:45:13.550 --> 00:45:14.550
I think, when you do intel assessments.

00:45:14.550 --> 00:45:15.550
QUESTION: Okay.

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:16.550
MS.

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:17.550
HARF: But I wouldn't read anything into the
fact that the assessment won't be coming,

00:45:17.550 --> 00:45:18.550
except at some point this week.

00:45:18.550 --> 00:45:19.550
I don't have more of a timeline.

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:20.550
QUESTION: So a postponement, not a cancelation?

00:45:20.550 --> 00:45:21.550
QUESTION: Oh --

00:45:21.550 --> 00:45:22.550
QUESTION: So just to be clear, the assessment
will be coming sometime or to be made public,

00:45:22.550 --> 00:45:23.550
but the President has got the assessment already?

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:24.550
MS.

00:45:24.550 --> 00:45:25.550
HARF: It's my -- yeah, that's my understanding.

00:45:25.550 --> 00:45:26.550
I don't want to speak for him on this.

00:45:26.550 --> 00:45:27.550
I want to make sure that that's actually 100
percent accurate, but it is my understanding,

00:45:27.550 --> 00:45:28.550
yes.

00:45:28.550 --> 00:45:29.550
It's my understanding that at this point we're
just preparing the classified assessment to

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:30.550
go to the Hill.

00:45:30.550 --> 00:45:31.550
But I don't want to get --

00:45:31.550 --> 00:45:32.550
QUESTION: Change topic?

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:33.550
QUESTION: Okay.

00:45:33.550 --> 00:45:34.550
But he's got the assessment?

00:45:34.550 --> 00:45:35.550
MS.

00:45:35.550 --> 00:45:36.550
HARF: Again, I don't know exactly about the
timing, and I don't want to get ahead of this.

00:45:36.550 --> 00:45:37.550
I'm not speaking for the IC here.

00:45:37.550 --> 00:45:38.550
So let me double-check, and if there's more
specifics to share with you, I can.

00:45:38.550 --> 00:45:39.550
QUESTION: And then what response would you
be seeking from the Hill?

00:45:39.550 --> 00:45:41.630
An agreement to go to attack or what kind
of -- what are you looking for from them?

00:45:41.630 --> 00:45:42.630
MS.

00:45:42.630 --> 00:45:46.910
HARF: Well, the Secretary and others have
been in close consultation with the Hill as

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:50.490
part of this -- of the process and talking
about a potential response.

00:45:50.490 --> 00:45:53.540
I don't have anything further for what that
consultation entails.

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:57.130
Clearly, we think it's important to talk to
Congress about this and to talk them through

00:45:57.130 --> 00:46:00.790
the assessment when we provide the classified
assessment as well.

00:46:00.790 --> 00:46:01.790
Yes.

00:46:01.790 --> 00:46:05.350
QUESTION: And then you've stated today that,
again, that the policy goal of the United

00:46:05.350 --> 00:46:07.530
States is to see a political transition --

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:08.530
MS.

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:09.530
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:46:09.530 --> 00:46:10.530
QUESTION: -- through a Geneva-like process.

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:13.900
Analysts and observers of this have said that
it's unlikely that Assad would come to the

00:46:13.900 --> 00:46:15.760
table while he views himself as winning.

00:46:15.760 --> 00:46:22.600
Wouldn't it be, for lack of a better word,
helpful if the response the U.S. takes turns

00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:27.240
the battlefield position and would reverse
his gains that he's made recently?

00:46:27.240 --> 00:46:28.240
MS.

00:46:28.240 --> 00:46:33.820
HARF: Well, I think it's fair to say that
every step we've taken to assist the opposition

00:46:33.820 --> 00:46:36.980
and help them grow in strength has been to
do just that, right?

00:46:36.980 --> 00:46:41.280
It's to help them grow stronger, to help them
coalesce, but also to help them be able to

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:42.490
make more gains on the ground.

00:46:42.490 --> 00:46:47.910
So I think that's been a broad goal from the
beginning and will continue to be, I'm sure,

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:49.490
with any eventual decision that the President
makes.

00:46:49.490 --> 00:46:53.910
QUESTION: So when we're looking at the response,
you've ruled out that this action would be

00:46:53.910 --> 00:46:55.440
aimed at regime change.

00:46:55.440 --> 00:46:56.440
MS.

00:46:56.440 --> 00:46:57.440
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:46:57.440 --> 00:47:01.430
QUESTION: Some have said that it could be
a specific response to chemical weapons use

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:03.180
and nothing further than that.

00:47:03.180 --> 00:47:09.440
But my question is: Is there an element of
it that would be aimed at helping change the

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:14.620
balance of power on the ground, or do you
view responding specifically to a chemical

00:47:14.620 --> 00:47:18.960
weapons attack as doing that as well?

00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:19.960
MS.

00:47:19.960 --> 00:47:25.870
HARF: I think they're probably just any action
-- any decision would probably meet both objectives.

00:47:25.870 --> 00:47:30.630
But again, no decision has been made yet,
so we're going to keep doing things that help

00:47:30.630 --> 00:47:32.530
the opposition grow and also respond to this.

00:47:32.530 --> 00:47:36.340
But again, I don't want to get ahead of what
the goals of a response would be before a

00:47:36.340 --> 00:47:39.220
decision on what that response is is actually
made.

00:47:39.220 --> 00:47:44.570
QUESTION: Marie, are you aware that most -- in
fact, if not all -- public polls show that

00:47:44.570 --> 00:47:48.430
the American people, by a very large majority,
oppose to any kind of intervention?

00:47:48.430 --> 00:47:50.310
Should that factor in in any kind of decision?

00:47:50.310 --> 00:47:51.310
MS.

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:55.600
HARF: I think the President's been clear that
he makes decisions about our national security

00:47:55.600 --> 00:48:00.000
based on what's best for national security
interests of this country, and I think it's

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:03.710
clear here that there are core national security
interests at stake for the United States.

00:48:03.710 --> 00:48:08.530
Clearly, the mass-scale use of chemical weapons
or a potential proliferation of these weapons

00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:13.330
flagrantly violates an important international
norm and therefore threatens American security.

00:48:13.330 --> 00:48:18.360
It's also worth keeping in mind that this
is taking place in a region that is already

00:48:18.360 --> 00:48:22.770
destabilized, and it is taking place in a
country that borders an ally of the United

00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:27.030
States in Turkey and a close friend and partner
of the United States in Jordan.

00:48:27.030 --> 00:48:30.670
So it's important that the United States and
the international community send a strong

00:48:30.670 --> 00:48:34.970
message, because it's in our national security
interests to do so, and that's what guides

00:48:34.970 --> 00:48:36.382
the President's decision making on these issues.

00:48:36.382 --> 00:48:37.382
QUESTION: Sorry.

00:48:37.382 --> 00:48:38.382
I missed one of the countries.

00:48:38.382 --> 00:48:39.382
Does Israel not matter at all?

00:48:39.382 --> 00:48:40.382
MS.

00:48:40.382 --> 00:48:41.382
HARF: Of course, Israel is our --

00:48:41.382 --> 00:48:42.382
QUESTION: Okay.

00:48:42.382 --> 00:48:43.382
And --

00:48:43.382 --> 00:48:44.382
MS.

00:48:44.382 --> 00:48:45.382
HARF: -- closest friend and ally in the region
and --

00:48:45.382 --> 00:48:46.382
QUESTION: But they don't -- they didn't merit
a mention in the talking points?

00:48:46.382 --> 00:48:47.382
MS.

00:48:47.382 --> 00:48:48.382
HARF: Clearly we will continue --

00:48:48.382 --> 00:48:49.382
QUESTION: And how about Lebanon?

00:48:49.382 --> 00:48:50.382
MS.

00:48:50.382 --> 00:49:41.860
HARF: -- to talk -- all of the countries.

00:49:41.860 --> 00:49:43.770
We have been clear that Syria and spill-out
violence from Syria has been destabilizing

00:49:43.770 --> 00:49:44.770
for the entire region, including Lebanon,
including Iraq.

00:49:44.770 --> 00:49:45.770
I just mentioned two countries here, but I've
talked at length about the disastrous effects

00:49:45.770 --> 00:49:46.770
that Syria is having on other countries in
the region as well.

00:49:46.770 --> 00:49:47.770
QUESTION: And you would argue as well -- and
the President would I assume, the Administration

00:49:47.770 --> 00:49:48.770
-- I assume everyone in the Administration
would argue that simply -- that quite apart

00:49:48.770 --> 00:49:49.770
from any interests that your allies -- the
interests of your allies in the region and

00:49:49.770 --> 00:49:50.770
quite apart from the threat of proliferation,
that the -- just the use and the death of

00:49:50.770 --> 00:49:51.770
Syrian civilians on such a mass scale is also
counter to U.S. national interests.

00:49:51.770 --> 00:49:52.770
Is that correct?

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:53.770
MS.

00:49:53.770 --> 00:49:54.770
HARF: Absolutely.

00:49:54.770 --> 00:49:55.770
QUESTION: All right.

00:49:55.770 --> 00:49:56.770
MS.

00:49:56.770 --> 00:49:57.770
HARF: And the continued chaos in Syria is
as well.

00:49:57.770 --> 00:49:58.770
Absolutely.

00:49:58.770 --> 00:49:59.770
QUESTION: Can we move on?

00:49:59.770 --> 00:50:00.770
QUESTION: Do you have a comment on the fact
that -- I'm sorry.

00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:01.770
QUESTION: No.

00:50:01.770 --> 00:50:02.770
I want to move to something else.

00:50:02.770 --> 00:50:03.770
QUESTION: Sorry, Matt.

00:50:03.770 --> 00:50:04.770
Go ahead.

00:50:04.770 --> 00:50:05.770
MS.

00:50:05.770 --> 00:50:06.770
HARF: Okay.

00:50:06.770 --> 00:50:07.770
Continue.

00:50:07.770 --> 00:50:08.770
We'll get to --

00:50:08.770 --> 00:50:09.770
QUESTION: No.

00:50:09.770 --> 00:50:10.770
I just wanted to ask you if you have any comment
on the statement by the Iraqi Government that

00:50:10.770 --> 00:50:11.770
they will not allow any kind of overflights,
be it by airplanes or fighter jets or cruise

00:50:11.770 --> 00:50:12.770
missiles, to go over their airspace into Syria
by the Americans.

00:50:12.770 --> 00:50:13.770
Are you aware of that?

00:50:13.770 --> 00:50:14.770
MS.

00:50:14.770 --> 00:50:15.770
HARF: Well, I actually haven't seen those
comments, Said, but I also don't want to -- don't

00:50:15.770 --> 00:50:16.770
think anyone should get ahead of where the
President is and whether or not a decision

00:50:16.770 --> 00:50:17.770
has been made and start talking about hypotheticals.

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:18.770
Anything else on Syria?

00:50:18.770 --> 00:50:19.770
QUESTION: On Syria.

00:50:19.770 --> 00:50:20.770
MS.

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:21.770
HARF: Yes.

00:50:21.770 --> 00:50:22.770
Mm-hmm.

00:50:22.770 --> 00:50:23.770
QUESTION: When did the assessment of additional
information that caused you to change your

00:50:23.770 --> 00:50:24.770
confidence level from very little down to
crystal clear -- when did that assessment

00:50:24.770 --> 00:50:25.770
come out?

00:50:25.770 --> 00:50:26.770
Was it this morning or last night?

00:50:26.770 --> 00:50:27.770
MS.

00:50:27.770 --> 00:50:28.770
HARF: Well, I wouldn't point to a specific
assessment.

00:50:28.770 --> 00:50:29.770
As I said, the intelligence community assessment
will be forthcoming publicly sometime this

00:50:29.770 --> 00:50:30.770
week.

00:50:30.770 --> 00:50:35.150
I think we've continued gathering information
and got to a point where we felt comfortable

00:50:35.150 --> 00:50:41.910
today publicly saying that the Syrian regime
is responsible for the use of chemical weapons

00:50:41.910 --> 00:50:42.960
in this situation.

00:50:42.960 --> 00:50:48.720
And again, from day one, common sense would
lead anybody to believe that, but we did our

00:50:48.720 --> 00:50:52.560
due diligence, and we're looking into it,
and more details will be coming from the intelligence

00:50:52.560 --> 00:50:53.560
community.

00:50:53.560 --> 00:50:59.690
QUESTION: But you can't point right now publicly
to what it was that got rid of that one scintilla

00:50:59.690 --> 00:51:00.730
of doubt from yesterday?

00:51:00.730 --> 00:51:01.730
MS.

00:51:01.730 --> 00:51:02.970
HARF: Well, I think that the preponderance
of evidence --

00:51:02.970 --> 00:51:03.970
QUESTION: Yeah, but that's --

00:51:03.970 --> 00:51:04.970
MS.

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:05.970
HARF: -- and again, the details of the intelligence
assessment --

00:51:05.970 --> 00:51:06.970
QUESTION: Right.

00:51:06.970 --> 00:51:07.970
MS.

00:51:07.970 --> 00:51:09.470
HARF: -- will be coming in the coming days,
but I think the fact that only one side in

00:51:09.470 --> 00:51:12.330
this conflict has the ability to use this
--

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:13.330
QUESTION: But the --

00:51:13.330 --> 00:51:14.330
MS.

00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:15.330
HARF: -- and one doesn't --

00:51:15.330 --> 00:51:16.330
QUESTION: Right.

00:51:16.330 --> 00:51:17.330
MS.

00:51:17.330 --> 00:51:18.330
HARF: -- is a huge part of that.

00:51:18.330 --> 00:51:19.330
But yes, the specifics on the assessments
will be coming this week.

00:51:19.330 --> 00:51:22.150
QUESTION: But the specific thing that made
the -- that got you over that last tick from

00:51:22.150 --> 00:51:28.750
11:59 to midnight of sure positivity will
-- you're not in a position to say what it

00:51:28.750 --> 00:51:29.750
was right now?

00:51:29.750 --> 00:51:30.750
MS.

00:51:30.750 --> 00:51:31.750
HARF: I'm not going to further --

00:51:31.750 --> 00:51:32.750
QUESTION: That that will be --

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:33.750
MS.

00:51:33.750 --> 00:51:34.750
HARF: -- detail the assessment.

00:51:34.750 --> 00:51:35.750
QUESTION: -- part of the assessment?

00:51:35.750 --> 00:51:36.750
MS.

00:51:36.750 --> 00:51:37.750
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:51:37.750 --> 00:51:38.750
QUESTION: Okay.

00:51:38.750 --> 00:51:39.750
Can I move on?

00:51:39.750 --> 00:51:40.750
I don't think you'll have an answer --

00:51:40.750 --> 00:51:41.750
QUESTION: Yeah.

00:51:41.750 --> 00:51:42.750
We'll move on.

00:51:42.750 --> 00:51:43.750
MS.

00:51:43.750 --> 00:51:44.750
HARF: Just one second.

00:51:44.750 --> 00:51:45.750
Yeah.

00:51:45.750 --> 00:51:46.750
Go -- on Syria still?

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:47.750
QUESTION: No, no.

00:51:47.750 --> 00:51:48.750
Not Syria.

00:51:48.750 --> 00:51:49.750
MS.

00:51:49.750 --> 00:51:50.750
HARF: Let's move on with Matt, and then we'll
get to you, Said.

00:51:50.750 --> 00:51:51.750
QUESTION: I just wanted to go to Egypt to
see how the -- is anyone pay attention to

00:51:51.750 --> 00:51:52.750
Egypt right now, or is everyone focused on
Syria?

00:51:52.750 --> 00:51:53.750
And if --

00:51:53.750 --> 00:51:54.750
MS.

00:51:54.750 --> 00:51:55.750
HARF: Absolutely.

00:51:55.750 --> 00:51:56.750
QUESTION: -- there are people paying attention
to Egypt, where -- what's the status of the

00:51:56.750 --> 00:51:57.750
review, the aid review, because we had more
people on the Hill calling for a suspension?

00:51:57.750 --> 00:51:58.750
MS.

00:51:58.750 --> 00:51:59.750
HARF: Well, clearly everybody is still very
focused on Egypt.

00:51:59.750 --> 00:52:00.820
I said that the Secretary spoke yesterday
with the Egyptian Foreign Minister, and quite

00:52:00.820 --> 00:52:05.640
frankly, in a lot of these discussions, Egypt
is discussed as well.

00:52:05.640 --> 00:52:07.380
Clearly everybody is focused on both.

00:52:07.380 --> 00:52:12.880
I have no update for you on timing of a decision
on the review of assistance at this point.

00:52:12.880 --> 00:52:17.970
QUESTION: Why is Egypt sort of conspicuously
absent from this discussion over the chemical

00:52:17.970 --> 00:52:18.992
weapons use in Syria?

00:52:18.992 --> 00:52:19.992
MS.

00:52:19.992 --> 00:52:20.992
HARF: In what way?

00:52:20.992 --> 00:52:21.992
QUESTION: In many ways.

00:52:21.992 --> 00:52:22.992
I mean, they're not --

00:52:22.992 --> 00:52:23.992
MS.

00:52:23.992 --> 00:52:24.992
HARF: How should it be part of this discussion?

00:52:24.992 --> 00:52:26.090
QUESTION: Are you discussing with them the
consequences of this chemical attack in Syria?

00:52:26.090 --> 00:52:27.090
MS.

00:52:27.090 --> 00:52:28.090
HARF: With the Egyptians?

00:52:28.090 --> 00:52:29.090
QUESTION: Yes.

00:52:29.090 --> 00:52:30.090
MS.

00:52:30.090 --> 00:52:31.200
HARF: Well, the Secretary spoke with the Egyptian
Foreign Minister yesterday.

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:35.850
I don't have a more fulsome readout of that
call, but clearly we're talking to all of

00:52:35.850 --> 00:52:40.900
our partners in the region about the use of
chemical weapons in Syria, also about the

00:52:40.900 --> 00:52:41.970
situation in Egypt.

00:52:41.970 --> 00:52:42.970
Mm-hmm.

00:52:42.970 --> 00:52:47.490
QUESTION: So you feel that Egypt is sort of
in lockstep with you on the issue of the chemical

00:52:47.490 --> 00:52:48.490
weapons in Syria?

00:52:48.490 --> 00:52:49.490
MS.

00:52:49.490 --> 00:52:50.490
HARF: That is not at all what I said.

00:52:50.490 --> 00:52:54.630
I'm not going to categorize the Egyptian interim
government's position on the chemical weapons

00:52:54.630 --> 00:52:55.630
use in Syria one way or the other.

00:52:55.630 --> 00:52:56.630
That's for them to characterize.

00:52:56.630 --> 00:52:57.630
QUESTION: Yeah.

00:52:57.630 --> 00:52:58.900
But does -- certainly Egypt is a very major
ally, and its position would mean a lot to

00:52:58.900 --> 00:53:00.640
you, wouldn't it?

00:53:00.640 --> 00:53:01.640
MS.

00:53:01.640 --> 00:53:06.480
HARF: Well, I think all of -- we take all
of our partners' positions seriously.

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:10.420
That's why the Secretary is engaged with them
on the phone on a regular basis.

00:53:10.420 --> 00:53:14.590
At the same time, Said, as you know, we are
currently reviewing our entire relationship

00:53:14.590 --> 00:53:18.760
with the Egyptian Government, including assistance,
and determining the best way to move that

00:53:18.760 --> 00:53:20.200
relationship forward.

00:53:20.200 --> 00:53:22.160
QUESTION: Just on that -- on his point --

00:53:22.160 --> 00:53:23.160
MS.

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:24.160
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:53:24.160 --> 00:53:28.090
QUESTION: Is -- are you looking for bilateral
-- for individual Arab countries to come out

00:53:28.090 --> 00:53:34.500
in support of a response, whatever it is,
or is the Arab League a blanket -- is that

00:53:34.500 --> 00:53:35.500
enough for you?

00:53:35.500 --> 00:53:36.500
MS.

00:53:36.500 --> 00:53:39.130
HARF: Well, certainly we believe that the
Arab League is an incredibly important organization

00:53:39.130 --> 00:53:40.520
to come out so publicly and strongly.

00:53:40.520 --> 00:53:41.520
QUESTION: But you would --

00:53:41.520 --> 00:53:42.520
MS.

00:53:42.520 --> 00:53:43.590
HARF: But we also would welcome, of course,
anybody --

00:53:43.590 --> 00:53:44.680
QUESTION: Individual countries --

00:53:44.680 --> 00:53:45.680
MS.

00:53:45.680 --> 00:53:48.900
HARF: Individual countries, anybody coming
out and condemning this use, and indicated

00:53:48.900 --> 00:53:51.640
that there should be accountability in this
situation, yes.

00:53:51.640 --> 00:53:52.640
QUESTION: Yes, please.

00:53:52.640 --> 00:53:53.640
MS.

00:53:53.640 --> 00:53:54.640
HARF: Yes.

00:53:54.640 --> 00:54:00.630
QUESTION: Regarding Egypt, it looks like that
the focusing on -- I mean, I know that Matt

00:54:00.630 --> 00:54:05.500
asked the same question, but somehow I try
to phrase it in a different way.

00:54:05.500 --> 00:54:06.690
Maybe I could --

00:54:06.690 --> 00:54:07.690
MS.

00:54:07.690 --> 00:54:09.380
HARF: Give it a shot.

00:54:09.380 --> 00:54:10.380
Let's try.

00:54:10.380 --> 00:54:14.800
QUESTION: -- get a different answer maybe,
as long as not hypothetical.

00:54:14.800 --> 00:54:20.130
It's like things are going on on the ground
and we are talking about here about the entire

00:54:20.130 --> 00:54:21.290
-- entirely reviewing the relations.

00:54:21.290 --> 00:54:22.290
MS.

00:54:22.290 --> 00:54:23.290
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:54:23.290 --> 00:54:28.800
QUESTION: Are you, I mean, trying to do anything
or just wait and see what's going on to happen

00:54:28.800 --> 00:54:33.500
and accordingly we decide what we can do,
I mean, from here to there?

00:54:33.500 --> 00:54:38.130
Because, I mean, things are going on on the
ground --

00:54:38.130 --> 00:54:39.130
MS.

00:54:39.130 --> 00:54:40.130
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:54:40.130 --> 00:54:46.440
QUESTION: -- and it's really clear that the
more decisive decisions are make by the interim

00:54:46.440 --> 00:54:53.619
government and more people are arrested, more
media outlets are closed, and so on and so

00:54:53.619 --> 00:54:54.619
on.

00:54:54.619 --> 00:54:59.690
Is there anything going on from this side
or just watching and waiting until let's finish

00:54:59.690 --> 00:55:05.580
-- it's our plateful of Syria now -- let's
finish this food and then we can find another

00:55:05.580 --> 00:55:06.580
thing later?

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:07.580
MS.

00:55:07.580 --> 00:55:08.580
HARF: Not at all.

00:55:08.580 --> 00:55:12.190
I think that everybody is still very focused
as well on what's happening on the ground

00:55:12.190 --> 00:55:13.190
in Egypt.

00:55:13.190 --> 00:55:15.460
That's playing into our ongoing review.

00:55:15.460 --> 00:55:20.750
Clearly, we're focused on Syria, too, but
nobody has stopped paying attention to what's

00:55:20.750 --> 00:55:21.750
going on on the ground.

00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:26.640
That's all part of what we're reviewing right
now and will feed into an eventual decision

00:55:26.640 --> 00:55:27.640
on what our

00:55:27.640 --> 00:55:28.730
relationship will look like going forward.

00:55:28.730 --> 00:55:32.740
QUESTION: Yeah, but the question is I know
that one of the favorite expression in this

00:55:32.740 --> 00:55:33.740
town is that I can walk and chew the --

00:55:33.740 --> 00:55:34.740
MS.

00:55:34.740 --> 00:55:35.790
HARF: I was going to say that --

00:55:35.790 --> 00:55:36.790
QUESTION: Okay.

00:55:36.790 --> 00:55:37.790
MS.

00:55:37.790 --> 00:55:38.790
HARF: -- but you beat me to it.

00:55:38.790 --> 00:55:41.630
QUESTION: I mean, it's one of this State Department
lexicon, you know what they call it.

00:55:41.630 --> 00:55:42.630
MS.

00:55:42.630 --> 00:55:43.630
HARF: Is it?

00:55:43.630 --> 00:55:44.630
Oh, I walked right into that one.

00:55:44.630 --> 00:55:45.630
Go ahead, yes.

00:55:45.630 --> 00:55:50.390
QUESTION: So I'm trying to figure out if you
are talking -- following what's going on in

00:55:50.390 --> 00:55:57.560
Egypt, what's your -- what is your observation,
what's your criteria, what you are doing?

00:55:57.560 --> 00:56:04.310
Because it seems that it's not exactly moving
in the same way that you were wishing or willing

00:56:04.310 --> 00:56:07.170
or participating or expecting.

00:56:07.170 --> 00:56:08.170
MS.

00:56:08.170 --> 00:56:12.560
HARF: Well, I think we've been clear that
the actions that the interim government has

00:56:12.560 --> 00:56:18.590
taken and continues to take in some cases
have forced us to reevaluate this entire relationship.

00:56:18.590 --> 00:56:22.820
And we've made it clear when they've taken
steps that aren't moving Egypt back towards

00:56:22.820 --> 00:56:25.380
an inclusive, sustainable democracy.

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:30.730
So obviously that's all playing into our assessment
and our review, and when we have announcement

00:56:30.730 --> 00:56:31.880
to make, we will make it.

00:56:31.880 --> 00:56:35.210
QUESTION: It's, I think -- to that point --

00:56:35.210 --> 00:56:36.210
MS.

00:56:36.210 --> 00:56:37.210
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:56:37.210 --> 00:56:40.830
QUESTION: -- is this -- are you in a process
of making a determination or completing some

00:56:40.830 --> 00:56:41.830
review?

00:56:41.830 --> 00:56:47.340
Or is this a dynamic, fluid situation where
it's not time to make such a determination?

00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:48.340
MS.

00:56:48.340 --> 00:56:51.130
HARF: We are in the process of undertaking
a review.

00:56:51.130 --> 00:56:55.830
Clearly, at some point the President will
make a decision about our broad relationship,

00:56:55.830 --> 00:56:59.550
but also about some of the assistance decisions
we've talked about in this room.

00:56:59.550 --> 00:57:02.940
So that review is ongoing; we're not in wait
and see mode.

00:57:02.940 --> 00:57:04.030
We're looking at all of that right now.

00:57:04.030 --> 00:57:05.030
Yes.

00:57:05.030 --> 00:57:07.710
QUESTION: I want to move on to the peace process,
but before I do I just want to remind you

00:57:07.710 --> 00:57:08.710
that neither is Israel nor Myanmar are signatories
to the chemical weapons treaty, so please

00:57:08.710 --> 00:57:09.710
--

00:57:09.710 --> 00:57:10.710
MS.

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:18.380
HARF: I will correct -- so both Israel and
Burma have signed, but have not yet ratified

00:57:18.380 --> 00:57:19.380
the CWC.

00:57:19.380 --> 00:57:20.380
QUESTION: They have not ratified.

00:57:20.380 --> 00:57:21.380
That's what I said.

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:22.380
MS.

00:57:22.380 --> 00:57:23.440
HARF: I said there were five countries only
that had not signed nor acceded to it.

00:57:23.440 --> 00:57:24.570
They have both signed it.

00:57:24.570 --> 00:57:25.570
QUESTION: Moving on to the peace process --

00:57:25.570 --> 00:57:26.570
MS.

00:57:26.570 --> 00:57:27.570
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:57:27.570 --> 00:57:28.570
QUESTION: -- is there anything that you can
share with us about the meeting yesterday?

00:57:28.570 --> 00:57:29.570
MS.

00:57:29.570 --> 00:57:30.570
HARF: I don't have any updates for you on
that.

00:57:30.570 --> 00:57:33.770
QUESTION: Can you tell us why the Palestinians
keep saying that they are boycotting, but

00:57:33.770 --> 00:57:35.650
then lo and behold, they did attend the meeting?

00:57:35.650 --> 00:57:36.650
MS.

00:57:36.650 --> 00:57:41.210
HARF: I'd refer you to the Palestinians to
explain their actions.

00:57:41.210 --> 00:57:42.210
QUESTION: Okay.

00:57:42.210 --> 00:57:45.400
Did anyone from this building or from elsewhere
talk to the Palestinians and say that you

00:57:45.400 --> 00:57:46.400
better go to the meetings?

00:57:46.400 --> 00:57:47.400
MS.

00:57:47.400 --> 00:57:50.060
HARF: I'm not going to give any more details
about our discussions with the parties in

00:57:50.060 --> 00:57:51.060
these negotiations.

00:57:51.060 --> 00:57:55.350
QUESTION: So not -- their threat to boycott
the meeting was not discussed by any American

00:57:55.350 --> 00:57:56.460
official with any Palestinian officials?

00:57:56.460 --> 00:57:57.460
MS.

00:57:57.460 --> 00:57:58.460
HARF: Absolutely not what I said, Said.

00:57:58.460 --> 00:58:02.369
I said I'm not going to discuss any of our
private discussions with either side as part

00:58:02.369 --> 00:58:03.369
of this negotiation.

00:58:03.369 --> 00:58:05.770
QUESTION: And lastly, when is the next round
expected?

00:58:05.770 --> 00:58:06.770
Are you aware?

00:58:06.770 --> 00:58:07.770
MS.

00:58:07.770 --> 00:58:08.770
HARF: I don't have any announcements for you
on that.

00:58:08.770 --> 00:58:10.840
QUESTION: This is something that the Secretary
remains focused on, though, correct?

00:58:10.840 --> 00:58:11.840
MS.

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:12.840
HARF: Absolutely.

00:58:12.840 --> 00:58:15.660
QUESTION: So Egypt, Syria, Israelis-Palestinians
-- how about the Arctic?

00:58:15.660 --> 00:58:16.660
MS.

00:58:16.660 --> 00:58:17.660
HARF: I think the Secretary --

00:58:17.660 --> 00:58:18.660
QUESTION: Whales?

00:58:18.660 --> 00:58:20.060
QUESTION: (Inaudible) with the Arctic?

00:58:20.060 --> 00:58:21.060
MS.

00:58:21.060 --> 00:58:22.390
HARF: The Secretary is --

00:58:22.390 --> 00:58:23.570
QUESTION: South China Sea?

00:58:23.570 --> 00:58:24.570
MS.

00:58:24.570 --> 00:58:27.430
HARF: -- remains fully engaged on the plethora
of issues that come to his plate every day,

00:58:27.430 --> 00:58:28.430
yes.

00:58:28.430 --> 00:58:29.430
QUESTION: And focused on all of them.

00:58:29.430 --> 00:58:30.430
MS.

00:58:30.430 --> 00:58:33.190
HARF: Clearly, Matt, he said that Syria this
week -- he was clear yesterday --

00:58:33.190 --> 00:58:34.190
QUESTION: Botswana?

00:58:34.190 --> 00:58:35.190
MS.

00:58:35.190 --> 00:58:37.780
HARF: -- that what had happened in Syria was
a serious priority and was a top priority.

00:58:37.780 --> 00:58:39.270
QUESTION: Can I just ask you, there was a
--

00:58:39.270 --> 00:58:40.270
MS.

00:58:40.270 --> 00:58:42.540
HARF: He has a plethora of interests and is
able to focus on a number of things at the

00:58:42.540 --> 00:58:43.540
same time.

00:58:43.540 --> 00:58:44.540
QUESTION: He's an extraordinary man.

00:58:44.540 --> 00:58:45.540
MS.

00:58:45.540 --> 00:58:46.540
HARF: Exactly.

00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:47.540
You took the words out of my mouth.

00:58:47.540 --> 00:58:48.540
It's amazing.

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:51.820
QUESTION: There was a report yesterday or
the day before, I think -- I can't remember

00:58:51.820 --> 00:58:55.980
when it was -- that said that the State Department
was saying now that the review -- the Keystone

00:58:55.980 --> 00:58:59.330
review would not be done until 2014.

00:58:59.330 --> 00:59:00.330
Is that correct?

00:59:00.330 --> 00:59:01.330
MS.

00:59:01.330 --> 00:59:04.350
HARF: I think we've been clear from the beginning
that we don't have a timeline.

00:59:04.350 --> 00:59:07.440
We haven't put a timeline on when the review
will be done.

00:59:07.440 --> 00:59:13.140
We are, as I said before, reviewing the application
in a rigorous, transparent, and efficient

00:59:13.140 --> 00:59:17.590
manner, but we've never put a timeline on
it and I don't have one for you now.

00:59:17.590 --> 00:59:20.330
QUESTION: Okay, but that's not entirely true.

00:59:20.330 --> 00:59:21.730
There was a timeline put on it before.

00:59:21.730 --> 00:59:25.390
I mean, people had talked about the EI -- I
mean, not a decision --

00:59:25.390 --> 00:59:26.390
MS.

00:59:26.390 --> 00:59:27.390
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:59:27.390 --> 00:59:28.390
QUESTION: -- but in terms of the EI -- in
terms of the Environmental Impact Statement

00:59:28.390 --> 00:59:33.030
and the re-do of the EIS after the route change
--

00:59:33.030 --> 00:59:34.030
MS.

00:59:34.030 --> 00:59:35.030
HARF: But what I'm referring to --

00:59:35.030 --> 00:59:36.030
QUESTION: -- is the decision.

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:37.030
MS.

00:59:37.030 --> 00:59:38.030
HARF: -- I think that's what the reports referred
to is the final.

00:59:38.030 --> 00:59:39.030
QUESTION: No, no.

00:59:39.030 --> 00:59:40.030
MS.

00:59:40.030 --> 00:59:41.030
HARF: The final decision.

00:59:41.030 --> 00:59:42.030
QUESTION: Well, maybe I misread it.

00:59:42.030 --> 00:59:43.880
But I thought it was about the -- I'm not
-- I realize that the final decision -- the

00:59:43.880 --> 00:59:45.300
President has delegated it, but will come
--

00:59:45.300 --> 00:59:46.300
MS.

00:59:46.300 --> 00:59:47.300
HARF: Mm-hmm.

00:59:47.300 --> 00:59:48.300
QUESTION: -- but I'm talking about the Environmental
Impact Statement.

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:49.300
MS.

00:59:49.300 --> 00:59:50.300
HARF: Yeah.

00:59:50.300 --> 00:59:51.300
The final environmental --

00:59:51.300 --> 00:59:52.300
QUESTION: So that --

00:59:52.300 --> 00:59:53.300
MS.

00:59:53.300 --> 00:59:55.690
HARF: -- again, will be released after additional
analysis in the issues identified in all of

00:59:55.690 --> 00:59:57.140
the public comments have been incorporated.

00:59:57.140 --> 00:59:58.140
QUESTION: Right.

00:59:58.140 --> 00:59:59.140
MS.

00:59:59.140 --> 01:00:00.140
HARF: That process is ongoing.

01:00:00.140 --> 01:00:01.440
I believe there's over 1.2 million, if that
number's correct.

01:00:01.440 --> 01:00:02.440
QUESTION: Something like that.

01:00:02.440 --> 01:00:03.440
MS.

01:00:03.440 --> 01:00:04.440
HARF: That takes a while.

01:00:04.440 --> 01:00:06.570
But it's my understanding that we haven't
put a timeline on that and aren't now.

01:00:06.570 --> 01:00:11.420
QUESTION: Do you have any idea how many of
the millions of -- or however many million

01:00:11.420 --> 01:00:13.150
comments it is have been gone through?

01:00:13.150 --> 01:00:16.420
I mean, do you know where in the process they
are?

01:00:16.420 --> 01:00:18.310
Are they still going through the comments?

01:00:18.310 --> 01:00:22.110
Have they gone through the comments and they're
incorporating them into the report now?

01:00:22.110 --> 01:00:23.110
Or is --

01:00:23.110 --> 01:00:24.110
MS.

01:00:24.110 --> 01:00:25.110
HARF: Well, let me get some more details for
you on that.

01:00:25.110 --> 01:00:26.110
I actually just don't know the answer.

01:00:26.110 --> 01:00:27.110
QUESTION: Okay.

01:00:27.110 --> 01:00:28.110
MS.

01:00:28.110 --> 01:00:29.110
HARF: I know there's a team of people looking
into this right now.

01:00:29.110 --> 01:00:30.110
I just don't know.

01:00:30.110 --> 01:00:31.110
QUESTION: All right.

01:00:31.110 --> 01:00:35.250
But it is not correct or it is correct that
the State Department has said that it won't

01:00:35.250 --> 01:00:36.550
be finished until 2014?

01:00:36.550 --> 01:00:37.550
MS.

01:00:37.550 --> 01:00:38.550
HARF: Not to my knowledge.

01:00:38.550 --> 01:00:39.550
Not to my knowledge.

01:00:39.550 --> 01:00:42.210
And again, I'm not putting a timeline on it
here.

01:00:42.210 --> 01:00:46.750
QUESTION: Okay, but a report that said that
you -- that the State Department has said

01:00:46.750 --> 01:00:48.960
that it won't be finished until 2014 would
not be correct; is that right?

01:00:48.960 --> 01:00:49.960
MS.

01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:51.280
HARF: I have -- that's my understanding, yes.

01:00:51.280 --> 01:00:52.580
I've certainly never said it.

01:00:52.580 --> 01:00:53.580
QUESTION: All right.

01:00:53.580 --> 01:00:54.580
Thank you.

01:00:54.580 --> 01:00:55.580
MS.

01:00:55.580 --> 01:00:55.583
HARF: Thank you.

